      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-run-lease-20260819",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 3000,
      "width": 848.4,
      "height": 1225.0,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#9d174d",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 618442907,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 771204331,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "boundElements": null,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (the RUN-SCOPED browser lease, keyed by a LEASE ID, and the two lifecycle\nsignals that make it possible).\n\nTHE DEFECT. Cortex dispatches ONE `tool.invoke` PER ACTION - `DaemonBrowserSession.dispatch` is\nreached from every act(), assert() and observe(). The executor acquired a ProfileLease per FRAME and\nreleased it in a `finally`, and release CLOSES the run's page and calls `clearCookies()` over the\nwhole jar. So a navigate landed, the page closed, the jar was wiped, and the next click ran on a\nfresh `about:blank` with no cookies. Every browser step after the first acted on a blank page: a\nmulti-step flow, which is the entire point of the capability, could not work. The per-action unit\ntests could not see it - they drive one action at a time, and the DISPATCH was always correct; the\nLIFETIME was wrong.\n\nTHE ROOT CAUSE WAS A MISSING SIGNAL, NOT A MISSING LOOP. `BrowserSession.dispose?()` was OPTIONAL and\n`DaemonBrowserSession` did not implement it, under a comment claiming the daemon \"needs no teardown\".\nWith no run-end signal on the wire, the end of each INVOKE was the only place teardown could hang.\n\nTHE KEY IS A LEASE ID, NOT THE RUN ID. Cortex mints one per execution PASS and threads it down the\ncall tree (`RunContext.browserLease`). Two shapes force it:\n  - A SUB-AUTOMATION is a separate run on the same owner - same daemon, same profile - executing\n    while its parent is blocked on it. Keyed by runId the child queued behind a lease the parent\n    could not release until the child returned: a deadlock, with the idle backstop then reaping the\n    waiting parent. Sharing the lease also puts the child on the page its parent left open.\n  - A RESUMED run (needs_credentials -> unlock -> continue) re-enters under the SAME runId. Ended\n    leases are tombstoned, so a per-run key would have refused every resumed pass forever.\n\nON THE WIRE (Rule 7, additive). `LocalBrowserStepInput` becomes TWO ARMS: a page step\n`{owner, leaseId?, action}` and a LIFECYCLE op `{owner, leaseId?, leaseOp}` where leaseOp is\n`release` or `keepalive`. The lifecycle verbs are NOT members of `LocalBrowserAction` and NOT members\nof Cortex's `PlaywrightAction`: everything in the action union is something a resolver, the planner\nor the vision tier can emit, so a lifecycle verb there would be one bad model completion away from a\nstep that ends its own run. They ride the ordinary browser envelope, so they pass BOTH gates and are\nledgered like any other step. `leaseId` is optional both ways: an older Cortex sends none and the\ndaemon falls back to the runId; an older daemon refuses the lifecycle arm at its zod boundary.\n\nTHREE WAYS A LEASE ENDS, AND ALL THREE ARE REQUIRED.\n  1. EXPLICIT - `leaseOp:'release'`, sent ONCE per call tree from the engine's run `finally` by the\n     pass that minted the lease (never by a sub-automation, which would tear the browser down under\n     its parent). It never throws - a throw there would replace the run's real outcome with a\n     teardown error - but it is never silent either: a release the daemon could not complete is\n     logged against the runId, because it means a session may still be resident.\n  2. IDLE BACKSTOP (2 min) - if Cortex dies, is killed, or its socket drops, the release never\n     arrives. This is a SECURITY control before it is hygiene: without it an authenticated Cofre\n     session stays resident in a jar the next automation on that profile inherits, and a headed\n     window stays open on somebody's desktop. The window stays SHORT only because Cortex heartbeats\n     every live lease (`leaseOp:'keepalive'`, every 45s): a live run legitimately goes minutes\n     without a browser step - blocked on a sub-automation, on a slow API call, or on a HUMAN at a\n     CAPTCHA in that very window - and a pure timer cannot tell those from an abandoned lease. The\n     timer is armed AFTER each step, never during one, so a slow step is never reaped out from under\n     itself, and a BUSY lease is never reaped at all.\n  3. SHUTDOWN - `closeAll` releases live leases BEFORE closing their contexts (on a PERSISTENT\n     profile the cookies are on disk, so closing a context out from under a held lease would leave\n     the session behind), and the SIGINT/SIGTERM handler AWAITS that teardown, bounded, before the\n     process is allowed to exit. The pidfile - the cross-process lock on the profile directory -\n     is held until the teardown finishes.\n\nSESSION LIFETIME IS NOW THE LEASE, EXACTLY. Cookies are injected ONCE at the first step and the Cofre\nsession is resolved once (a thunk, so a warm lease never re-reads credential material); the jar is\ncleared at the end and at nothing else. THE WIPE IS LOUD: `clearCookies` throwing propagates to the\nexecutor, which ledgers `error` and answers a failed step, because a run told it ended cleanly while\nits session is still resident is the worst outcome available. Teardown order is localStorage (needs a\nlive page), then the page (so nothing can be handed a Set-Cookie after the wipe), then the jar.\n\nTWO FRAMES ARE OWNER-SCOPED LIKE EVERY OTHER FRAME. `runs` is a process-wide map and a lease id is an\nopaque string, so the profile is resolved for EVERY browser frame, lifecycle ones included: a frame\ncarrying owner A cannot end a lease taken on owner B's profile.\n\nA REAPED LEASE IS REFUSED, NOT RESTARTED. A late invoke for an ended lease is answered by name from a\nbounded tombstone list. Serving it a fresh lease would recreate the original defect - a blank page\nand an empty jar, reported to Cortex as a step that ran. Because a lease id belongs to one PASS, a\ntombstone can never refuse a resumed run.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (the RUN-SCOPED browser lease, keyed by a LEASE ID, and the two lifecycle\nsignals that make it possible).\n\nTHE DEFECT. Cortex dispatches ONE `tool.invoke` PER ACTION - `DaemonBrowserSession.dispatch` is\nreached from every act(), assert() and observe(). The executor acquired a ProfileLease per FRAME and\nreleased it in a `finally`, and release CLOSES the run's page and calls `clearCookies()` over the\nwhole jar. So a navigate landed, the page closed, the jar was wiped, and the next click ran on a\nfresh `about:blank` with no cookies. Every browser step after the first acted on a blank page: a\nmulti-step flow, which is the entire point of the capability, could not work. The per-action unit\ntests could not see it - they drive one action at a time, and the DISPATCH was always correct; the\nLIFETIME was wrong.\n\nTHE ROOT CAUSE WAS A MISSING SIGNAL, NOT A MISSING LOOP. `BrowserSession.dispose?()` was OPTIONAL and\n`DaemonBrowserSession` did not implement it, under a comment claiming the daemon \"needs no teardown\".\nWith no run-end signal on the wire, the end of each INVOKE was the only place teardown could hang.\n\nTHE KEY IS A LEASE ID, NOT THE RUN ID. Cortex mints one per execution PASS and threads it down the\ncall tree (`RunContext.browserLease`). Two shapes force it:\n  - A SUB-AUTOMATION is a separate run on the same owner - same daemon, same profile - executing\n    while its parent is blocked on it. Keyed by runId the child queued behind a lease the parent\n    could not release until the child returned: a deadlock, with the idle backstop then reaping the\n    waiting parent. Sharing the lease also puts the child on the page its parent left open.\n  - A RESUMED run (needs_credentials -> unlock -> continue) re-enters under the SAME runId. Ended\n    leases are tombstoned, so a per-run key would have refused every resumed pass forever.\n\nON THE WIRE (Rule 7, additive). `LocalBrowserStepInput` becomes TWO ARMS: a page step\n`{owner, leaseId?, action}` and a LIFECYCLE op `{owner, leaseId?, leaseOp}` where leaseOp is\n`release` or `keepalive`. The lifecycle verbs are NOT members of `LocalBrowserAction` and NOT members\nof Cortex's `PlaywrightAction`: everything in the action union is something a resolver, the planner\nor the vision tier can emit, so a lifecycle verb there would be one bad model completion away from a\nstep that ends its own run. They ride the ordinary browser envelope, so they pass BOTH gates and are\nledgered like any other step. `leaseId` is optional both ways: an older Cortex sends none and the\ndaemon falls back to the runId; an older daemon refuses the lifecycle arm at its zod boundary.\n\nTHREE WAYS A LEASE ENDS, AND ALL THREE ARE REQUIRED.\n  1. EXPLICIT - `leaseOp:'release'`, sent ONCE per call tree from the engine's run `finally` by the\n     pass that minted the lease (never by a sub-automation, which would tear the browser down under\n     its parent). It never throws - a throw there would replace the run's real outcome with a\n     teardown error - but it is never silent either: a release the daemon could not complete is\n     logged against the runId, because it means a session may still be resident.\n  2. IDLE BACKSTOP (2 min) - if Cortex dies, is killed, or its socket drops, the release never\n     arrives. This is a SECURITY control before it is hygiene: without it an authenticated Cofre\n     session stays resident in a jar the next automation on that profile inherits, and a headed\n     window stays open on somebody's desktop. The window stays SHORT only because Cortex heartbeats\n     every live lease (`leaseOp:'keepalive'`, every 45s): a live run legitimately goes minutes\n     without a browser step - blocked on a sub-automation, on a slow API call, or on a HUMAN at a\n     CAPTCHA in that very window - and a pure timer cannot tell those from an abandoned lease. The\n     timer is armed AFTER each step, never during one, so a slow step is never reaped out from under\n     itself, and a BUSY lease is never reaped at all.\n  3. SHUTDOWN - `closeAll` releases live leases BEFORE closing their contexts (on a PERSISTENT\n     profile the cookies are on disk, so closing a context out from under a held lease would leave\n     the session behind), and the SIGINT/SIGTERM handler AWAITS that teardown, bounded, before the\n     process is allowed to exit. The pidfile - the cross-process lock on the profile directory -\n     is held until the teardown finishes.\n\nSESSION LIFETIME IS NOW THE LEASE, EXACTLY. Cookies are injected ONCE at the first step and the Cofre\nsession is resolved once (a thunk, so a warm lease never re-reads credential material); the jar is\ncleared at the end and at nothing else. THE WIPE IS LOUD: `clearCookies` throwing propagates to the\nexecutor, which ledgers `error` and answers a failed step, because a run told it ended cleanly while\nits session is still resident is the worst outcome available. Teardown order is localStorage (needs a\nlive page), then the page (so nothing can be handed a Set-Cookie after the wipe), then the jar.\n\nTWO FRAMES ARE OWNER-SCOPED LIKE EVERY OTHER FRAME. `runs` is a process-wide map and a lease id is an\nopaque string, so the profile is resolved for EVERY browser frame, lifecycle ones included: a frame\ncarrying owner A cannot end a lease taken on owner B's profile.\n\nA REAPED LEASE IS REFUSED, NOT RESTARTED. A late invoke for an ended lease is answered by name from a\nbounded tombstone list. Serving it a fresh lease would recreate the original defect - a blank page\nand an empty jar, reported to Cortex as a step that ran. Because a lease id belongs to one PASS, a\ntombstone can never refuse a resumed run.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (the RUN-SCOPED browser lease, keyed by a LEASE ID, and the two lifecycle\nsignals that make it possible).\n\nTHE DEFECT. Cortex dispatches ONE `tool.invoke` PER ACTION - `DaemonBrowserSession.dispatch` is\nreached from every act(), assert() and observe(). The executor acquired a ProfileLease per FRAME and\nreleased it in a `finally`, and release CLOSES the run's page and calls `clearCookies()` over the\nwhole jar. So a navigate landed, the page closed, the jar was wiped, and the next click ran on a\nfresh `about:blank` with no cookies. Every browser step after the first acted on a blank page: a\nmulti-step flow, which is the entire point of the capability, could not work. The per-action unit\ntests could not see it - they drive one action at a time, and the DISPATCH was always correct; the\nLIFETIME was wrong.\n\nTHE ROOT CAUSE WAS A MISSING SIGNAL, NOT A MISSING LOOP. `BrowserSession.dispose?()` was OPTIONAL and\n`DaemonBrowserSession` did not implement it, under a comment claiming the daemon \"needs no teardown\".\nWith no run-end signal on the wire, the end of each INVOKE was the only place teardown could hang.\n\nTHE KEY IS A LEASE ID, NOT THE RUN ID. Cortex mints one per execution PASS and threads it down the\ncall tree (`RunContext.browserLease`). Two shapes force it:\n  - A SUB-AUTOMATION is a separate run on the same owner - same daemon, same profile - executing\n    while its parent is blocked on it. Keyed by runId the child queued behind a lease the parent\n    could not release until the child returned: a deadlock, with the idle backstop then reaping the\n    waiting parent. Sharing the lease also puts the child on the page its parent left open.\n  - A RESUMED run (needs_credentials -> unlock -> continue) re-enters under the SAME runId. Ended\n    leases are tombstoned, so a per-run key would have refused every resumed pass forever.\n\nON THE WIRE (Rule 7, additive). `LocalBrowserStepInput` becomes TWO ARMS: a page step\n`{owner, leaseId?, action}` and a LIFECYCLE op `{owner, leaseId?, leaseOp}` where leaseOp is\n`release` or `keepalive`. The lifecycle verbs are NOT members of `LocalBrowserAction` and NOT members\nof Cortex's `PlaywrightAction`: everything in the action union is something a resolver, the planner\nor the vision tier can emit, so a lifecycle verb there would be one bad model completion away from a\nstep that ends its own run. They ride the ordinary browser envelope, so they pass BOTH gates and are\nledgered like any other step. `leaseId` is optional both ways: an older Cortex sends none and the\ndaemon falls back to the runId; an older daemon refuses the lifecycle arm at its zod boundary.\n\nTHREE WAYS A LEASE ENDS, AND ALL THREE ARE REQUIRED.\n  1. EXPLICIT - `leaseOp:'release'`, sent ONCE per call tree from the engine's run `finally` by the\n     pass that minted the lease (never by a sub-automation, which would tear the browser down under\n     its parent). It never throws - a throw there would replace the run's real outcome with a\n     teardown error - but it is never silent either: a release the daemon could not complete is\n     logged against the runId, because it means a session may still be resident.\n  2. IDLE BACKSTOP (2 min) - if Cortex dies, is killed, or its socket drops, the release never\n     arrives. This is a SECURITY control before it is hygiene: without it an authenticated Cofre\n     session stays resident in a jar the next automation on that profile inherits, and a headed\n     window stays open on somebody's desktop. The window stays SHORT only because Cortex heartbeats\n     every live lease (`leaseOp:'keepalive'`, every 45s): a live run legitimately goes minutes\n     without a browser step - blocked on a sub-automation, on a slow API call, or on a HUMAN at a\n     CAPTCHA in that very window - and a pure timer cannot tell those from an abandoned lease. The\n     timer is armed AFTER each step, never during one, so a slow step is never reaped out from under\n     itself, and a BUSY lease is never reaped at all.\n  3. SHUTDOWN - `closeAll` releases live leases BEFORE closing their contexts (on a PERSISTENT\n     profile the cookies are on disk, so closing a context out from under a held lease would leave\n     the session behind), and the SIGINT/SIGTERM handler AWAITS that teardown, bounded, before the\n     process is allowed to exit. The pidfile - the cross-process lock on the profile directory -\n     is held until the teardown finishes.\n\nSESSION LIFETIME IS NOW THE LEASE, EXACTLY. Cookies are injected ONCE at the first step and the Cofre\nsession is resolved once (a thunk, so a warm lease never re-reads credential material); the jar is\ncleared at the end and at nothing else. THE WIPE IS LOUD: `clearCookies` throwing propagates to the\nexecutor, which ledgers `error` and answers a failed step, because a run told it ended cleanly while\nits session is still resident is the worst outcome available. Teardown order is localStorage (needs a\nlive page), then the page (so nothing can be handed a Set-Cookie after the wipe), then the jar.\n\nTWO FRAMES ARE OWNER-SCOPED LIKE EVERY OTHER FRAME. `runs` is a process-wide map and a lease id is an\nopaque string, so the profile is resolved for EVERY browser frame, lifecycle ones included: a frame\ncarrying owner A cannot end a lease taken on owner B's profile.\n\nA REAPED LEASE IS REFUSED, NOT RESTARTED. A late invoke for an ended lease is answered by name from a\nbounded tombstone list. Serving it a fresh lease would recreate the original defect - a blank page\nand an empty jar, reported to Cortex as a step that ran. Because a lease id belongs to one PASS, a\ntombstone can never refuse a resumed run."
