      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4: execution LOCALITY is wired, and a session prefers the machine it was made on).\n\nWHAT WAS INERT. `automation/egress-policy.ts` implemented the route-out decision exactly - requirement,\noffline policy, org-scoped candidate selection, `proxyOptionFor` - and shipped with ZERO callers. Nothing\never read a run's `StepTarget` or `offlinePolicy`. Meanwhile the browser-vs-bridge choice was made by ONE\nGLOBAL FLAG, `config.localBrowserEnabled`, defaulting to `!isProd`: outside production EVERY browser step\nsilently ran in the hosted Chromium, portals that score datacenter IPs included. Production only looked\ncorrect because the flag happened to be off there.\n\nTHE GATE IS NOW THE ORIGIN POSTURE, IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT. New `automation/locality.ts` (pure; no store, no\nseam, no env) answers bridge | in-process | blocked from the posture `origin-posture.ts` resolves at use,\nthe step's declaration, the daemon connection and the org's fleet. Permissive may be carried by the hosted\nbrowser; adversarial never is; an origin nobody classified is adversarial, so every automation authored\nbefore posture existed is bridge-only. `localBrowserEnabled` survives as an operator KILL SWITCH only.\n\nTHE WIRING. The run loop resolves locality per step, for the four step types that can reach a browser\n(navigate/wait/browser/verify) - an api_call or integration step has no locality and is never halted by one.\n`LocalBrowserContextProvider` gains the resolved egress, because the proxy is a `newContext` LAUNCH option\nand cannot be applied afterwards; `server.ts` renders it with `proxyOptionFor` and binds the fleet listing\nto `bridge/registry.ts` `egressCandidatesForOrg` through a new `EgressCandidateResolver` seam whose unbound\ndefault is EMPTY - and empty refuses.\n\nPREFERENTIAL BRIDGE (P4.2), NO NEW IDENTITY PRIMITIVE. `sessionMetadata.establishedBy.pairingId` has been\nstamped by `bridge/attended.ts` since the ceremony landed and nothing read it. `ensureSession` now reports\nit, and `credential-gate.ts` turns it into a preference for ADVERSARIAL origins only: that machine or wait,\nnever a colleague's - another household on another ASN is as foreign to the portal as a datacenter. A\npermissive credential is portable and carries no preference at all (`kind: 'any'`).\n\nTHE BLOCKED CHANNEL. `TriggerRunOutcome.outcome` gains `blocked` (awaiting_daemon | needs_credentials);\n`mapAutomationOutcome` maps it; `recordOutcome` makes it NEUTRAL against the 20-strike FAILURE_CEILING - it\nneither resets nor increments, because a fire waiting on its owner says nothing about whether the schedule\nworks. Counting it auto-paused a working schedule after twenty shut-laptop nights; resetting it would let a\nbroken one hide behind the occasional block. A blocked fire NOTIFIES its owner over the per-user\nnotifications channel (`schedule_blocked`, code-only - no server prose), because neutral outcomes are\notherwise silent. What it never does is fall back to a datacenter route for an adversarial target.\n\nNOT A NEW RunStatus. `awaiting_daemon` already means \"a machine of yours is needed\", is already threaded\nthrough the SSE union, the reload-recovery set and the UI; blocked is the SCHEDULE rail's word for it.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4: execution LOCALITY is wired, and a session prefers the machine it was made on).\n\nWHAT WAS INERT. `automation/egress-policy.ts` implemented the route-out decision exactly - requirement,\noffline policy, org-scoped candidate selection, `proxyOptionFor` - and shipped with ZERO callers. Nothing\never read a run's `StepTarget` or `offlinePolicy`. Meanwhile the browser-vs-bridge choice was made by ONE\nGLOBAL FLAG, `config.localBrowserEnabled`, defaulting to `!isProd`: outside production EVERY browser step\nsilently ran in the hosted Chromium, portals that score datacenter IPs included. Production only looked\ncorrect because the flag happened to be off there.\n\nTHE GATE IS NOW THE ORIGIN POSTURE, IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT. New `automation/locality.ts` (pure; no store, no\nseam, no env) answers bridge | in-process | blocked from the posture `origin-posture.ts` resolves at use,\nthe step's declaration, the daemon connection and the org's fleet. Permissive may be carried by the hosted\nbrowser; adversarial never is; an origin nobody classified is adversarial, so every automation authored\nbefore posture existed is bridge-only. `localBrowserEnabled` survives as an operator KILL SWITCH only.\n\nTHE WIRING. The run loop resolves locality per step, for the four step types that can reach a browser\n(navigate/wait/browser/verify) - an api_call or integration step has no locality and is never halted by one.\n`LocalBrowserContextProvider` gains the resolved egress, because the proxy is a `newContext` LAUNCH option\nand cannot be applied afterwards; `server.ts` renders it with `proxyOptionFor` and binds the fleet listing\nto `bridge/registry.ts` `egressCandidatesForOrg` through a new `EgressCandidateResolver` seam whose unbound\ndefault is EMPTY - and empty refuses.\n\nPREFERENTIAL BRIDGE (P4.2), NO NEW IDENTITY PRIMITIVE. `sessionMetadata.establishedBy.pairingId` has been\nstamped by `bridge/attended.ts` since the ceremony landed and nothing read it. `ensureSession` now reports\nit, and `credential-gate.ts` turns it into a preference for ADVERSARIAL origins only: that machine or wait,\nnever a colleague's - another household on another ASN is as foreign to the portal as a datacenter. A\npermissive credential is portable and carries no preference at all (`kind: 'any'`).\n\nTHE BLOCKED CHANNEL. `TriggerRunOutcome.outcome` gains `blocked` (awaiting_daemon | needs_credentials);\n`mapAutomationOutcome` maps it; `recordOutcome` makes it NEUTRAL against the 20-strike FAILURE_CEILING - it\nneither resets nor increments, because a fire waiting on its owner says nothing about whether the schedule\nworks. Counting it auto-paused a working schedule after twenty shut-laptop nights; resetting it would let a\nbroken one hide behind the occasional block. A blocked fire NOTIFIES its owner over the per-user\nnotifications channel (`schedule_blocked`, code-only - no server prose), because neutral outcomes are\notherwise silent. What it never does is fall back to a datacenter route for an adversarial target.\n\nNOT A NEW RunStatus. `awaiting_daemon` already means \"a machine of yours is needed\", is already threaded\nthrough the SSE union, the reload-recovery set and the UI; blocked is the SCHEDULE rail's word for it.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4: execution LOCALITY is wired, and a session prefers the machine it was made on).\n\nWHAT WAS INERT. `automation/egress-policy.ts` implemented the route-out decision exactly - requirement,\noffline policy, org-scoped candidate selection, `proxyOptionFor` - and shipped with ZERO callers. Nothing\never read a run's `StepTarget` or `offlinePolicy`. Meanwhile the browser-vs-bridge choice was made by ONE\nGLOBAL FLAG, `config.localBrowserEnabled`, defaulting to `!isProd`: outside production EVERY browser step\nsilently ran in the hosted Chromium, portals that score datacenter IPs included. Production only looked\ncorrect because the flag happened to be off there.\n\nTHE GATE IS NOW THE ORIGIN POSTURE, IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT. New `automation/locality.ts` (pure; no store, no\nseam, no env) answers bridge | in-process | blocked from the posture `origin-posture.ts` resolves at use,\nthe step's declaration, the daemon connection and the org's fleet. Permissive may be carried by the hosted\nbrowser; adversarial never is; an origin nobody classified is adversarial, so every automation authored\nbefore posture existed is bridge-only. `localBrowserEnabled` survives as an operator KILL SWITCH only.\n\nTHE WIRING. The run loop resolves locality per step, for the four step types that can reach a browser\n(navigate/wait/browser/verify) - an api_call or integration step has no locality and is never halted by one.\n`LocalBrowserContextProvider` gains the resolved egress, because the proxy is a `newContext` LAUNCH option\nand cannot be applied afterwards; `server.ts` renders it with `proxyOptionFor` and binds the fleet listing\nto `bridge/registry.ts` `egressCandidatesForOrg` through a new `EgressCandidateResolver` seam whose unbound\ndefault is EMPTY - and empty refuses.\n\nPREFERENTIAL BRIDGE (P4.2), NO NEW IDENTITY PRIMITIVE. `sessionMetadata.establishedBy.pairingId` has been\nstamped by `bridge/attended.ts` since the ceremony landed and nothing read it. `ensureSession` now reports\nit, and `credential-gate.ts` turns it into a preference for ADVERSARIAL origins only: that machine or wait,\nnever a colleague's - another household on another ASN is as foreign to the portal as a datacenter. A\npermissive credential is portable and carries no preference at all (`kind: 'any'`).\n\nTHE BLOCKED CHANNEL. `TriggerRunOutcome.outcome` gains `blocked` (awaiting_daemon | needs_credentials);\n`mapAutomationOutcome` maps it; `recordOutcome` makes it NEUTRAL against the 20-strike FAILURE_CEILING - it\nneither resets nor increments, because a fire waiting on its owner says nothing about whether the schedule\nworks. Counting it auto-paused a working schedule after twenty shut-laptop nights; resetting it would let a\nbroken one hide behind the occasional block. A blocked fire NOTIFIES its owner over the per-user\nnotifications channel (`schedule_blocked`, code-only - no server prose), because neutral outcomes are\notherwise silent. What it never does is fall back to a datacenter route for an adversarial target.\n\nNOT A NEW RunStatus. `awaiting_daemon` already means \"a machine of yours is needed\", is already threaded\nthrough the SSE union, the reload-recovery set and the UI; blocked is the SCHEDULE rail's word for it."
