      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 CORRECTIONS: the order of the decision, and the cap the neutrality removed).\n\nTHE ORDER IS THE SECURITY PROPERTY. The run loop used to call the CREDENTIAL GATE first and resolve\nLOCALITY after it. The gate fires on nothing more than a step declaring `credentialRefs`, and its\n`ensureSession` typist path OPENS THE HOSTED CHROMIUM - via `defaultOpenBrowser`, which reached the\nbrowser seam with no route argument at all, i.e. the datacenter - and submits a password there. So a\nstep naming a Cofre item against a portal nobody had classified would type a real credential into an\nadversarial origin from a datacenter IP, before the code that exists to forbid that had run.\nLocality now resolves FIRST, and a step it refuses never reaches the gate at all.\n\nTWO LOCKS ON THE UNATTENDED TYPIST, both closed by default, neither sufficient alone:\n  1. THE RUN LOOP'S PERMIT (`CredentialGateInput.hostedBrowser`) - this process has a hosted browser\n     for this step, by this route. Absent when the operator kill switch is off.\n  2. POSTURE (`classification.cloudEgressAllowed`) - only ever true for a `permissive` declaration,\n     by the frozen constructor in origin-posture.ts.\nOnly both together produce `EnsureSessionInput.hostedTypist`, which is PRESENCE-IS-PERMISSION: with\nno permit the typist route becomes a `needs-human` refusal with `attempted: false`, before anything\nis unwrapped, navigated to or typed. The permit also CARRIES THE ROUTE, so a permitted login leaves\nby the door the run resolved rather than always the datacenter. THE CITIUS RAIL IS THE ONE EXPLICIT\nEXCEPTION - it calls ensureSession directly, takes no part in the locality decision, and passes the\npermit unconditionally to keep the behaviour it shipped with. Named in findings.md, not grandfathered.\n\nTHE CEILING KEPT ITS CAP. \"A blocked fire is neutral against FAILURE_CEILING\" was too broad. The test\nis not blocked-vs-failed but DOES WAITING FIX IT. `awaiting_daemon` is about the ENVIRONMENT - the\nlaptop opens and the next fire succeeds with nobody touching the schedule - so it stays neutral in\nboth directions. `needs_credentials` and `awaiting_consent` are blocked on a HUMAN ACT, and repeating\nthem IS the hazard: a portal password changes, the nightly fire routes to the typist under a standing\ngrant, submits, meets the wrong-password signature, and does it again every night forever against a\nportal with an unknown lock-out policy. Those keep driving the ceiling, and the ceiling is the\nper-schedule cap. `TriggerRunOutcome.code` carries WHICH block, the badge derives its words from that\ncode, and an unnamed block counts - not knowing is not a known-safe answer.\n\nA RETIRED CEREMONY MACHINE GETS A WAY OUT. `preferredPairingId` is read off a stored session and never\nrevised, so retiring the laptop that established it blocked the automation forever on hardware nobody\nowns, naming a pairing UUID no surface ever shows a user. The fleet listing distinguishes GONE from\nASLEEP, and the refusal now names the act that fixes it - establish this session again, from a machine\nyou still have. It does NOT fall through to another machine: that is the substitution P4.2 forbids,\nand the ceremony machine being retired does not make it less of one.\n\nAND A POSTURE LICENSES ONE ORIGIN. A hosted session observed to have drifted off the origin its\ndeclaration was about carries no further steps. It stops the steps AFTER the drift, never the act that\ndrifts - named as a limitation rather than sold as a boundary.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 CORRECTIONS: the order of the decision, and the cap the neutrality removed).\n\nTHE ORDER IS THE SECURITY PROPERTY. The run loop used to call the CREDENTIAL GATE first and resolve\nLOCALITY after it. The gate fires on nothing more than a step declaring `credentialRefs`, and its\n`ensureSession` typist path OPENS THE HOSTED CHROMIUM - via `defaultOpenBrowser`, which reached the\nbrowser seam with no route argument at all, i.e. the datacenter - and submits a password there. So a\nstep naming a Cofre item against a portal nobody had classified would type a real credential into an\nadversarial origin from a datacenter IP, before the code that exists to forbid that had run.\nLocality now resolves FIRST, and a step it refuses never reaches the gate at all.\n\nTWO LOCKS ON THE UNATTENDED TYPIST, both closed by default, neither sufficient alone:\n  1. THE RUN LOOP'S PERMIT (`CredentialGateInput.hostedBrowser`) - this process has a hosted browser\n     for this step, by this route. Absent when the operator kill switch is off.\n  2. POSTURE (`classification.cloudEgressAllowed`) - only ever true for a `permissive` declaration,\n     by the frozen constructor in origin-posture.ts.\nOnly both together produce `EnsureSessionInput.hostedTypist`, which is PRESENCE-IS-PERMISSION: with\nno permit the typist route becomes a `needs-human` refusal with `attempted: false`, before anything\nis unwrapped, navigated to or typed. The permit also CARRIES THE ROUTE, so a permitted login leaves\nby the door the run resolved rather than always the datacenter. THE CITIUS RAIL IS THE ONE EXPLICIT\nEXCEPTION - it calls ensureSession directly, takes no part in the locality decision, and passes the\npermit unconditionally to keep the behaviour it shipped with. Named in findings.md, not grandfathered.\n\nTHE CEILING KEPT ITS CAP. \"A blocked fire is neutral against FAILURE_CEILING\" was too broad. The test\nis not blocked-vs-failed but DOES WAITING FIX IT. `awaiting_daemon` is about the ENVIRONMENT - the\nlaptop opens and the next fire succeeds with nobody touching the schedule - so it stays neutral in\nboth directions. `needs_credentials` and `awaiting_consent` are blocked on a HUMAN ACT, and repeating\nthem IS the hazard: a portal password changes, the nightly fire routes to the typist under a standing\ngrant, submits, meets the wrong-password signature, and does it again every night forever against a\nportal with an unknown lock-out policy. Those keep driving the ceiling, and the ceiling is the\nper-schedule cap. `TriggerRunOutcome.code` carries WHICH block, the badge derives its words from that\ncode, and an unnamed block counts - not knowing is not a known-safe answer.\n\nA RETIRED CEREMONY MACHINE GETS A WAY OUT. `preferredPairingId` is read off a stored session and never\nrevised, so retiring the laptop that established it blocked the automation forever on hardware nobody\nowns, naming a pairing UUID no surface ever shows a user. The fleet listing distinguishes GONE from\nASLEEP, and the refusal now names the act that fixes it - establish this session again, from a machine\nyou still have. It does NOT fall through to another machine: that is the substitution P4.2 forbids,\nand the ceremony machine being retired does not make it less of one.\n\nAND A POSTURE LICENSES ONE ORIGIN. A hosted session observed to have drifted off the origin its\ndeclaration was about carries no further steps. It stops the steps AFTER the drift, never the act that\ndrifts - named as a limitation rather than sold as a boundary.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 CORRECTIONS: the order of the decision, and the cap the neutrality removed).\n\nTHE ORDER IS THE SECURITY PROPERTY. The run loop used to call the CREDENTIAL GATE first and resolve\nLOCALITY after it. The gate fires on nothing more than a step declaring `credentialRefs`, and its\n`ensureSession` typist path OPENS THE HOSTED CHROMIUM - via `defaultOpenBrowser`, which reached the\nbrowser seam with no route argument at all, i.e. the datacenter - and submits a password there. So a\nstep naming a Cofre item against a portal nobody had classified would type a real credential into an\nadversarial origin from a datacenter IP, before the code that exists to forbid that had run.\nLocality now resolves FIRST, and a step it refuses never reaches the gate at all.\n\nTWO LOCKS ON THE UNATTENDED TYPIST, both closed by default, neither sufficient alone:\n  1. THE RUN LOOP'S PERMIT (`CredentialGateInput.hostedBrowser`) - this process has a hosted browser\n     for this step, by this route. Absent when the operator kill switch is off.\n  2. POSTURE (`classification.cloudEgressAllowed`) - only ever true for a `permissive` declaration,\n     by the frozen constructor in origin-posture.ts.\nOnly both together produce `EnsureSessionInput.hostedTypist`, which is PRESENCE-IS-PERMISSION: with\nno permit the typist route becomes a `needs-human` refusal with `attempted: false`, before anything\nis unwrapped, navigated to or typed. The permit also CARRIES THE ROUTE, so a permitted login leaves\nby the door the run resolved rather than always the datacenter. THE CITIUS RAIL IS THE ONE EXPLICIT\nEXCEPTION - it calls ensureSession directly, takes no part in the locality decision, and passes the\npermit unconditionally to keep the behaviour it shipped with. Named in findings.md, not grandfathered.\n\nTHE CEILING KEPT ITS CAP. \"A blocked fire is neutral against FAILURE_CEILING\" was too broad. The test\nis not blocked-vs-failed but DOES WAITING FIX IT. `awaiting_daemon` is about the ENVIRONMENT - the\nlaptop opens and the next fire succeeds with nobody touching the schedule - so it stays neutral in\nboth directions. `needs_credentials` and `awaiting_consent` are blocked on a HUMAN ACT, and repeating\nthem IS the hazard: a portal password changes, the nightly fire routes to the typist under a standing\ngrant, submits, meets the wrong-password signature, and does it again every night forever against a\nportal with an unknown lock-out policy. Those keep driving the ceiling, and the ceiling is the\nper-schedule cap. `TriggerRunOutcome.code` carries WHICH block, the badge derives its words from that\ncode, and an unnamed block counts - not knowing is not a known-safe answer.\n\nA RETIRED CEREMONY MACHINE GETS A WAY OUT. `preferredPairingId` is read off a stored session and never\nrevised, so retiring the laptop that established it blocked the automation forever on hardware nobody\nowns, naming a pairing UUID no surface ever shows a user. The fleet listing distinguishes GONE from\nASLEEP, and the refusal now names the act that fixes it - establish this session again, from a machine\nyou still have. It does NOT fall through to another machine: that is the substitution P4.2 forbids,\nand the ceremony machine being retired does not make it less of one.\n\nAND A POSTURE LICENSES ONE ORIGIN. A hosted session observed to have drifted off the origin its\ndeclaration was about carries no further steps. It stops the steps AFTER the drift, never the act that\ndrifts - named as a limitation rather than sold as a boundary."
