      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND THREE: the wiring is pinned, and two refusals were wrong).\n\nWHAT ROUND TWO LEFT UNDONE. It closed the blockers and pinned NONE of them: an adversarial verifier\nmutated each new safety property and every suite stayed green. Each is now driven through the real\nengine, and each was confirmed by MUTATION - break it, watch the suite go red, restore.\n\nTHE CEREMONY PREFERENCE IS TWO HALVES, pinned separately because either can be deleted alone.\n  A. `resolveLocalityForStep` forwards `preferredPairingId` into `resolveLocality`.\n  B. the run loop RE-RESOLVES the verdict after the credential gate discovers that preference.\nThe half-A case gates an INTEGRATION step - no locality of its own, so no re-resolution can fire -\nand observes the preference on the NEXT step. The half-B case gates the browser step itself. Delete\nB and only the B case reddens; the A case stays green. A third case connects the ceremony machine\nand expects the run to COMPLETE, so neither pin is satisfiable by an engine that refuses everything.\n\nTHE HOSTED-BROWSER PERMIT IS THE PRODUCTION POSTURE RULE. `localBrowserEnabled` gates whether the\ngate is offered a hosted browser at all; making the permit unconditional left every suite green, on\nthe one condition that stops a password being typed into a hosted Chromium in production.\n\nTHE PERMIT DROPPED THE ROUTE ON EVERY BRIDGE STEP - and the comment on that line said it did not.\nThe comment was right. `bridge` and `in-process` BOTH carry an EgressResolution; only `in-process`\nwas forwarded. For a permissive origin whose step is pinned or declares residential egress, the\nWORK ran on the machine's line while the typist's LOGIN left by the datacenter. Same portal, same\nsession, two doors - performed by the one act that types a password.\n\nA RETIRED CEREMONY MACHINE IS NOW TERMINAL. `awaiting_daemon` is NEUTRAL against the failure ceiling\nbecause opening a laptop clears it - true of a machine that is OFF, false of one that is GONE. The\nretirement inherited that neutrality and re-fired nightly, forever, uncounted. The blocked verdict\nnow carries a REQUIRED `clearedBy: machine | human`; `human` halts in `needs_credentials`, which is\nalready non-neutral, already blocked-not-failed, and already deep-links to the Cofre. Required, so a\nnew refusal cannot inherit \"retry forever\" by saying nothing. A census pins that the retirement is\nthe ONLY `human` answer - making them all terminal would auto-pause a schedule for a shut laptop.\n\nTHE BADGE'S CODE HAD TO REACH IT FROM THE PAGE. The component was correct and tested; nothing\npinned the two PAGES passing `code`. Dropped, every blocked fire reads \"Aguarda aprovacao\" again -\nan approval that does not exist. Both surfaces pinned separately, on the words a person reads.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND THREE: the wiring is pinned, and two refusals were wrong).\n\nWHAT ROUND TWO LEFT UNDONE. It closed the blockers and pinned NONE of them: an adversarial verifier\nmutated each new safety property and every suite stayed green. Each is now driven through the real\nengine, and each was confirmed by MUTATION - break it, watch the suite go red, restore.\n\nTHE CEREMONY PREFERENCE IS TWO HALVES, pinned separately because either can be deleted alone.\n  A. `resolveLocalityForStep` forwards `preferredPairingId` into `resolveLocality`.\n  B. the run loop RE-RESOLVES the verdict after the credential gate discovers that preference.\nThe half-A case gates an INTEGRATION step - no locality of its own, so no re-resolution can fire -\nand observes the preference on the NEXT step. The half-B case gates the browser step itself. Delete\nB and only the B case reddens; the A case stays green. A third case connects the ceremony machine\nand expects the run to COMPLETE, so neither pin is satisfiable by an engine that refuses everything.\n\nTHE HOSTED-BROWSER PERMIT IS THE PRODUCTION POSTURE RULE. `localBrowserEnabled` gates whether the\ngate is offered a hosted browser at all; making the permit unconditional left every suite green, on\nthe one condition that stops a password being typed into a hosted Chromium in production.\n\nTHE PERMIT DROPPED THE ROUTE ON EVERY BRIDGE STEP - and the comment on that line said it did not.\nThe comment was right. `bridge` and `in-process` BOTH carry an EgressResolution; only `in-process`\nwas forwarded. For a permissive origin whose step is pinned or declares residential egress, the\nWORK ran on the machine's line while the typist's LOGIN left by the datacenter. Same portal, same\nsession, two doors - performed by the one act that types a password.\n\nA RETIRED CEREMONY MACHINE IS NOW TERMINAL. `awaiting_daemon` is NEUTRAL against the failure ceiling\nbecause opening a laptop clears it - true of a machine that is OFF, false of one that is GONE. The\nretirement inherited that neutrality and re-fired nightly, forever, uncounted. The blocked verdict\nnow carries a REQUIRED `clearedBy: machine | human`; `human` halts in `needs_credentials`, which is\nalready non-neutral, already blocked-not-failed, and already deep-links to the Cofre. Required, so a\nnew refusal cannot inherit \"retry forever\" by saying nothing. A census pins that the retirement is\nthe ONLY `human` answer - making them all terminal would auto-pause a schedule for a shut laptop.\n\nTHE BADGE'S CODE HAD TO REACH IT FROM THE PAGE. The component was correct and tested; nothing\npinned the two PAGES passing `code`. Dropped, every blocked fire reads \"Aguarda aprovacao\" again -\nan approval that does not exist. Both surfaces pinned separately, on the words a person reads.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND THREE: the wiring is pinned, and two refusals were wrong).\n\nWHAT ROUND TWO LEFT UNDONE. It closed the blockers and pinned NONE of them: an adversarial verifier\nmutated each new safety property and every suite stayed green. Each is now driven through the real\nengine, and each was confirmed by MUTATION - break it, watch the suite go red, restore.\n\nTHE CEREMONY PREFERENCE IS TWO HALVES, pinned separately because either can be deleted alone.\n  A. `resolveLocalityForStep` forwards `preferredPairingId` into `resolveLocality`.\n  B. the run loop RE-RESOLVES the verdict after the credential gate discovers that preference.\nThe half-A case gates an INTEGRATION step - no locality of its own, so no re-resolution can fire -\nand observes the preference on the NEXT step. The half-B case gates the browser step itself. Delete\nB and only the B case reddens; the A case stays green. A third case connects the ceremony machine\nand expects the run to COMPLETE, so neither pin is satisfiable by an engine that refuses everything.\n\nTHE HOSTED-BROWSER PERMIT IS THE PRODUCTION POSTURE RULE. `localBrowserEnabled` gates whether the\ngate is offered a hosted browser at all; making the permit unconditional left every suite green, on\nthe one condition that stops a password being typed into a hosted Chromium in production.\n\nTHE PERMIT DROPPED THE ROUTE ON EVERY BRIDGE STEP - and the comment on that line said it did not.\nThe comment was right. `bridge` and `in-process` BOTH carry an EgressResolution; only `in-process`\nwas forwarded. For a permissive origin whose step is pinned or declares residential egress, the\nWORK ran on the machine's line while the typist's LOGIN left by the datacenter. Same portal, same\nsession, two doors - performed by the one act that types a password.\n\nA RETIRED CEREMONY MACHINE IS NOW TERMINAL. `awaiting_daemon` is NEUTRAL against the failure ceiling\nbecause opening a laptop clears it - true of a machine that is OFF, false of one that is GONE. The\nretirement inherited that neutrality and re-fired nightly, forever, uncounted. The blocked verdict\nnow carries a REQUIRED `clearedBy: machine | human`; `human` halts in `needs_credentials`, which is\nalready non-neutral, already blocked-not-failed, and already deep-links to the Cofre. Required, so a\nnew refusal cannot inherit \"retry forever\" by saying nothing. A census pins that the retirement is\nthe ONLY `human` answer - making them all terminal would auto-pause a schedule for a shut laptop.\n\nTHE BADGE'S CODE HAD TO REACH IT FROM THE PAGE. The component was correct and tested; nothing\npinned the two PAGES passing `code`. Dropped, every blocked fire reads \"Aguarda aprovacao\" again -\nan approval that does not exist. Both surfaces pinned separately, on the words a person reads."
