      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND FOUR: the preference is scoped to its ORIGIN, the bridge names the\nmachine it will RUN on, and the last mile became bindable).\n\nTHE PATTERN THE FIRST THREE ROUNDS SHARED. Each fixed what the review named, and the same underlying\nerror resurfaced somewhere adjacent: A VALUE RESOLVED IN ONE CONTEXT AND APPLIED IN ANOTHER. Round\nfour fixes that shape, and fixes it structurally where a structure was available.\n\n1. THE CEREMONY PREFERENCE WAS RUN-SCOPED WHEN IT IS ORIGIN-SCOPED.\n   `preferredPairingId` was ONE run-level `let`, set by whichever gated step last reported a pairing,\n   forwarded into EVERY later browser step whatever origin that step was about. A session is bound to\n   ONE portal, so a run touching two portals judged portal B against portal A's ceremony machine.\n   Retire that machine and portal B's step halted `needs_credentials` NAMING PORTAL B, asking the\n   owner to establish that session again. They could - correctly, repeatedly - and the next fire\n   produced the identical halt, while `needs_credentials` (deliberately not neutral) drove the\n   failure ceiling to the 20-strike auto-pause. The lesser variant, the ceremony machine merely\n   asleep, is the same misdirection wearing the neutral halt.\n   THE FIX IS THE TYPE. The gate emits `preferredPairing: { origin, pairingId }` - one value, so a\n   pairing cannot be held without the portal it is about - and the run loop files them in\n   `preferredPairingByOrigin`, read back per step from the origin THAT step resolved. A miss means\n   \"any machine of yours\", never another portal's ceremony machine.\n\n2. THE SET DECIDING WHICH STEPS ARE JUDGED AT ALL WAS PINNED BY NOTHING.\n   `STEP_TYPES_NEEDING_BROWSER` mutated to `new Set(['wait'])` left 94 files / 1324 tests green:\n   every locality fixture drove a `wait` step, so `navigate`, `browser` and `verify` - the three that\n   actually drive a page - were covered by nothing. The fixtures now drive one case per member, in\n   both directions (the P4.1 posture refusal and the P4.2 re-judgement after the gate), and each\n   removal is verified to redden its own case. The observable is the halt MESSAGE, never the status:\n   a step waved past locality falls through to the executor's \"there is no browser here\", which ends\n   the run in the SAME `awaiting_daemon` with the same empty context log.\n\n3. A BRIDGE VERDICT CARRIED `resolveEgress`'s PICK, NOT THE MACHINE THE WORK RUNS ON.\n   Same error as (1), different shape. `resolveEgress` answers \"which machine should the HOSTED\n   browser proxy through\" and takes `usable[0]` when no pairing is named. With `pair_office` listed\n   first and `pair_home` dialled in, the verdict named `pair_office` while every byte of work left\n   from `pair_home` - and the one consumer routes the hosted typist's LOGIN through that field. Same\n   portal, same session, TWO DIFFERENT DOORS, diverging at the one act that hands over a secret.\n   `bridgeEgressFor` resolves against `daemonPairingId` and nothing else, and does NOT consult the\n   offline policy: `queue` has nothing to wait for and `datacenter` is exactly the wrong door, so the\n   only outcomes are `machine` or `refused`.\n   `hostedTypistPermitFor` is the second half - ONE RULE, THE LOGIN LEAVES BY THE SAME DOOR AS THE\n   WORK. In-process types through its own route. A bridge types through the connected machine's line\n   when it has one; through the ordinary hosted browser when the step required nothing of its route\n   (the origin is permissive by construction); and THROUGH NOTHING when a residential line was\n   required and the connected machine cannot lend one - the permit is WITHHELD and the run halts\n   asking for a person, because `proxyOptionFor` answers undefined for a refused resolution and the\n   old shape therefore opened a plain datacenter context and typed the password into it.\n\n4. THE LAST MILE HAD NO TEST BECAUSE IT HAD NO SEAM.\n   The provider turning a resolved route into actually-proxied traffic was an inline closure in\n   `server.ts` reaching straight for `getSharedBrowser()`. Nothing could bind it, so nothing\n   exercised it: gutting it to `return browser.newContext()` - every residential run silently leaving\n   from the datacenter - was caught by no suite. Now `seams.ts`\n   `localBrowserContextProviderUsing(openBrowser)`, with the composition root doing nothing but\n   binding `getSharedBrowser` into it. The direct import WAS the defect.\n\n5. THE `clearedBy` CENSUS IS NOW A CENSUS.\n   Five hand-written inputs under a docblock claiming to cover every refusal; a new\n   `clearedBy: 'human'` branch could be added, reached, and leave it green. It now walks the cross\n   product of the whole input space, collects every refusal actually emitted, and asserts the set:\n   the `human` answers are exactly the retirement, the FULL set is exactly the one enumerated, and no\n   refusal answers both ways. A census only catches what its space REACHES - a branch conditioned on\n   more than two fleet candidates was missed by the first space, so the space carries a larger fleet.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND FOUR: the preference is scoped to its ORIGIN, the bridge names the\nmachine it will RUN on, and the last mile became bindable).\n\nTHE PATTERN THE FIRST THREE ROUNDS SHARED. Each fixed what the review named, and the same underlying\nerror resurfaced somewhere adjacent: A VALUE RESOLVED IN ONE CONTEXT AND APPLIED IN ANOTHER. Round\nfour fixes that shape, and fixes it structurally where a structure was available.\n\n1. THE CEREMONY PREFERENCE WAS RUN-SCOPED WHEN IT IS ORIGIN-SCOPED.\n   `preferredPairingId` was ONE run-level `let`, set by whichever gated step last reported a pairing,\n   forwarded into EVERY later browser step whatever origin that step was about. A session is bound to\n   ONE portal, so a run touching two portals judged portal B against portal A's ceremony machine.\n   Retire that machine and portal B's step halted `needs_credentials` NAMING PORTAL B, asking the\n   owner to establish that session again. They could - correctly, repeatedly - and the next fire\n   produced the identical halt, while `needs_credentials` (deliberately not neutral) drove the\n   failure ceiling to the 20-strike auto-pause. The lesser variant, the ceremony machine merely\n   asleep, is the same misdirection wearing the neutral halt.\n   THE FIX IS THE TYPE. The gate emits `preferredPairing: { origin, pairingId }` - one value, so a\n   pairing cannot be held without the portal it is about - and the run loop files them in\n   `preferredPairingByOrigin`, read back per step from the origin THAT step resolved. A miss means\n   \"any machine of yours\", never another portal's ceremony machine.\n\n2. THE SET DECIDING WHICH STEPS ARE JUDGED AT ALL WAS PINNED BY NOTHING.\n   `STEP_TYPES_NEEDING_BROWSER` mutated to `new Set(['wait'])` left 94 files / 1324 tests green:\n   every locality fixture drove a `wait` step, so `navigate`, `browser` and `verify` - the three that\n   actually drive a page - were covered by nothing. The fixtures now drive one case per member, in\n   both directions (the P4.1 posture refusal and the P4.2 re-judgement after the gate), and each\n   removal is verified to redden its own case. The observable is the halt MESSAGE, never the status:\n   a step waved past locality falls through to the executor's \"there is no browser here\", which ends\n   the run in the SAME `awaiting_daemon` with the same empty context log.\n\n3. A BRIDGE VERDICT CARRIED `resolveEgress`'s PICK, NOT THE MACHINE THE WORK RUNS ON.\n   Same error as (1), different shape. `resolveEgress` answers \"which machine should the HOSTED\n   browser proxy through\" and takes `usable[0]` when no pairing is named. With `pair_office` listed\n   first and `pair_home` dialled in, the verdict named `pair_office` while every byte of work left\n   from `pair_home` - and the one consumer routes the hosted typist's LOGIN through that field. Same\n   portal, same session, TWO DIFFERENT DOORS, diverging at the one act that hands over a secret.\n   `bridgeEgressFor` resolves against `daemonPairingId` and nothing else, and does NOT consult the\n   offline policy: `queue` has nothing to wait for and `datacenter` is exactly the wrong door, so the\n   only outcomes are `machine` or `refused`.\n   `hostedTypistPermitFor` is the second half - ONE RULE, THE LOGIN LEAVES BY THE SAME DOOR AS THE\n   WORK. In-process types through its own route. A bridge types through the connected machine's line\n   when it has one; through the ordinary hosted browser when the step required nothing of its route\n   (the origin is permissive by construction); and THROUGH NOTHING when a residential line was\n   required and the connected machine cannot lend one - the permit is WITHHELD and the run halts\n   asking for a person, because `proxyOptionFor` answers undefined for a refused resolution and the\n   old shape therefore opened a plain datacenter context and typed the password into it.\n\n4. THE LAST MILE HAD NO TEST BECAUSE IT HAD NO SEAM.\n   The provider turning a resolved route into actually-proxied traffic was an inline closure in\n   `server.ts` reaching straight for `getSharedBrowser()`. Nothing could bind it, so nothing\n   exercised it: gutting it to `return browser.newContext()` - every residential run silently leaving\n   from the datacenter - was caught by no suite. Now `seams.ts`\n   `localBrowserContextProviderUsing(openBrowser)`, with the composition root doing nothing but\n   binding `getSharedBrowser` into it. The direct import WAS the defect.\n\n5. THE `clearedBy` CENSUS IS NOW A CENSUS.\n   Five hand-written inputs under a docblock claiming to cover every refusal; a new\n   `clearedBy: 'human'` branch could be added, reached, and leave it green. It now walks the cross\n   product of the whole input space, collects every refusal actually emitted, and asserts the set:\n   the `human` answers are exactly the retirement, the FULL set is exactly the one enumerated, and no\n   refusal answers both ways. A census only catches what its space REACHES - a branch conditioned on\n   more than two fleet candidates was missed by the first space, so the space carries a larger fleet.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND FOUR: the preference is scoped to its ORIGIN, the bridge names the\nmachine it will RUN on, and the last mile became bindable).\n\nTHE PATTERN THE FIRST THREE ROUNDS SHARED. Each fixed what the review named, and the same underlying\nerror resurfaced somewhere adjacent: A VALUE RESOLVED IN ONE CONTEXT AND APPLIED IN ANOTHER. Round\nfour fixes that shape, and fixes it structurally where a structure was available.\n\n1. THE CEREMONY PREFERENCE WAS RUN-SCOPED WHEN IT IS ORIGIN-SCOPED.\n   `preferredPairingId` was ONE run-level `let`, set by whichever gated step last reported a pairing,\n   forwarded into EVERY later browser step whatever origin that step was about. A session is bound to\n   ONE portal, so a run touching two portals judged portal B against portal A's ceremony machine.\n   Retire that machine and portal B's step halted `needs_credentials` NAMING PORTAL B, asking the\n   owner to establish that session again. They could - correctly, repeatedly - and the next fire\n   produced the identical halt, while `needs_credentials` (deliberately not neutral) drove the\n   failure ceiling to the 20-strike auto-pause. The lesser variant, the ceremony machine merely\n   asleep, is the same misdirection wearing the neutral halt.\n   THE FIX IS THE TYPE. The gate emits `preferredPairing: { origin, pairingId }` - one value, so a\n   pairing cannot be held without the portal it is about - and the run loop files them in\n   `preferredPairingByOrigin`, read back per step from the origin THAT step resolved. A miss means\n   \"any machine of yours\", never another portal's ceremony machine.\n\n2. THE SET DECIDING WHICH STEPS ARE JUDGED AT ALL WAS PINNED BY NOTHING.\n   `STEP_TYPES_NEEDING_BROWSER` mutated to `new Set(['wait'])` left 94 files / 1324 tests green:\n   every locality fixture drove a `wait` step, so `navigate`, `browser` and `verify` - the three that\n   actually drive a page - were covered by nothing. The fixtures now drive one case per member, in\n   both directions (the P4.1 posture refusal and the P4.2 re-judgement after the gate), and each\n   removal is verified to redden its own case. The observable is the halt MESSAGE, never the status:\n   a step waved past locality falls through to the executor's \"there is no browser here\", which ends\n   the run in the SAME `awaiting_daemon` with the same empty context log.\n\n3. A BRIDGE VERDICT CARRIED `resolveEgress`'s PICK, NOT THE MACHINE THE WORK RUNS ON.\n   Same error as (1), different shape. `resolveEgress` answers \"which machine should the HOSTED\n   browser proxy through\" and takes `usable[0]` when no pairing is named. With `pair_office` listed\n   first and `pair_home` dialled in, the verdict named `pair_office` while every byte of work left\n   from `pair_home` - and the one consumer routes the hosted typist's LOGIN through that field. Same\n   portal, same session, TWO DIFFERENT DOORS, diverging at the one act that hands over a secret.\n   `bridgeEgressFor` resolves against `daemonPairingId` and nothing else, and does NOT consult the\n   offline policy: `queue` has nothing to wait for and `datacenter` is exactly the wrong door, so the\n   only outcomes are `machine` or `refused`.\n   `hostedTypistPermitFor` is the second half - ONE RULE, THE LOGIN LEAVES BY THE SAME DOOR AS THE\n   WORK. In-process types through its own route. A bridge types through the connected machine's line\n   when it has one; through the ordinary hosted browser when the step required nothing of its route\n   (the origin is permissive by construction); and THROUGH NOTHING when a residential line was\n   required and the connected machine cannot lend one - the permit is WITHHELD and the run halts\n   asking for a person, because `proxyOptionFor` answers undefined for a refused resolution and the\n   old shape therefore opened a plain datacenter context and typed the password into it.\n\n4. THE LAST MILE HAD NO TEST BECAUSE IT HAD NO SEAM.\n   The provider turning a resolved route into actually-proxied traffic was an inline closure in\n   `server.ts` reaching straight for `getSharedBrowser()`. Nothing could bind it, so nothing\n   exercised it: gutting it to `return browser.newContext()` - every residential run silently leaving\n   from the datacenter - was caught by no suite. Now `seams.ts`\n   `localBrowserContextProviderUsing(openBrowser)`, with the composition root doing nothing but\n   binding `getSharedBrowser` into it. The direct import WAS the defect.\n\n5. THE `clearedBy` CENSUS IS NOW A CENSUS.\n   Five hand-written inputs under a docblock claiming to cover every refusal; a new\n   `clearedBy: 'human'` branch could be added, reached, and leave it green. It now walks the cross\n   product of the whole input space, collects every refusal actually emitted, and asserts the set:\n   the `human` answers are exactly the retirement, the FULL set is exactly the one enumerated, and no\n   refusal answers both ways. A census only catches what its space REACHES - a branch conditioned on\n   more than two fleet candidates was missed by the first space, so the space carries a larger fleet."
