      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SIX: an EMPTY fleet is a fact, not ignorance - and four pieces of dead\nsurface come out).\n\nTHE REGRESSION THIS ROUND EXISTS FOR. Rounds three to five taught the run loop to tell a machine that\nis ASLEEP from one that is GONE, and made `awaiting_daemon` NEUTRAL against the 20-strike failure\nceiling on the grounds that opening a laptop clears it. Put those together for a SOLO TENANT who\npairs one laptop, holds an attended ceremony on it, then revokes the pairing:\n\n  `egressCandidatesForOrg` filters `revokedAt: null`, so the org's listing is genuinely `[]`\n    -> `[]` was read as \"this process does not know what this org has\"\n    -> `machineRetired` answered NO, so NEITHER retirement branch fired\n    -> no daemon connected, so the run halted `awaiting_daemon`\n    -> `awaiting_daemon` is NEUTRAL, so the schedule re-fired nightly, for ever, uncounted,\n       telling the owner to connect a machine that no longer existed.\n\nOn `main` that same case mapped to `failed` and auto-paused after 20 fires. The branch turned a\nBOUNDED dead end into an UNBOUNDED one - the exact pathology its own retirement fix removes.\n\nTHE FIX IS ONE DISTINCTION, APPLIED IN THREE PLACES. The `EgressCandidateResolver` seam answers\n`EgressCandidate[] | null`: `null` = NO LISTING (the UNBOUND default; ignorance, which may never\nescalate a neutral wait into a terminal halt), `[]` = THE REGISTRY SAYS THIS ORG HAS NO MACHINES.\n`machineRetired(id, null)` is false and `machineRetired(id, [])` is true. `resolveLocality` turns a\n\"start your machine\" refusal into a TERMINAL one when the listing is known-empty and no daemon is\nconnected, because there is nothing to start. A store error still THROWS rather than answering\n`null`: a throw is a terminal run failure and therefore bounded.\n\n`clearedBy` NOW NAMES THE ACT, NOT THE ACTOR: `'start-a-machine' | 'pair-a-machine'`. Its one\nconsumer has to pick a halt with it, and \"a person must do something\" was never enough to pick one.\n`pair-a-machine` becomes the ceremony halt when the step DECLARES a credential (the solo-tenant case:\npair a machine, then establish the session) and a plain non-recoverable failure when it does not - a\ncredential ask for a step that wants none is a wrong specific instruction, which is worse than an\nhonest general one.\n\nWHAT CAME OUT, all of it reachable only from a test:\n - `resolveLocality`'s copy of the retirement refusal. A preference is LEARNED only from a checkout\n   that SUCCEEDED, and a ceremony session is bound to its machine's residential line, so a successful\n   checkout proves the machine is still listed. `credentialGateRecord` is the only producer now, and\n   `SESSION_MACHINE_RETIRED_REASON` moved to `egress-policy.ts` beside the predicate that produces it.\n - `establishedByPairingId` on `EnsureSessionResult.reestablished`, read off `EnsureSessionInput.\n   vantage`, which has no production supplier and cannot get one while the typist is hosted.\n - the `schedule_blocked` notification's silence: emitted for every blocked fire, subscribed by\n   nothing in `web/`. The schedules page now listens, toasts the CAUSE's words and refetches.\n - `hostedTypist: {}` hard-coded in the Citius rail - the one consumer able to type a court password\n   into the hosted Chromium for an origin nobody classified. The permit is an INPUT now, composed at\n   `routes/sync.ts` from `classifyOrigin`, which withholds it.\n\nAND ONE TENANCY REPAIR. `resolveEgress` had degraded to narrowing its ROWS by a set of pairing IDS.\nA foreign row sharing one of our ids, listed first, became `usable[0]` and its tailnet address became\nthe run's proxy. `c.org === actorOrg` is back on the row filter: a boundary must not rest on an id\nbeing unique across tenants.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SIX: an EMPTY fleet is a fact, not ignorance - and four pieces of dead\nsurface come out).\n\nTHE REGRESSION THIS ROUND EXISTS FOR. Rounds three to five taught the run loop to tell a machine that\nis ASLEEP from one that is GONE, and made `awaiting_daemon` NEUTRAL against the 20-strike failure\nceiling on the grounds that opening a laptop clears it. Put those together for a SOLO TENANT who\npairs one laptop, holds an attended ceremony on it, then revokes the pairing:\n\n  `egressCandidatesForOrg` filters `revokedAt: null`, so the org's listing is genuinely `[]`\n    -> `[]` was read as \"this process does not know what this org has\"\n    -> `machineRetired` answered NO, so NEITHER retirement branch fired\n    -> no daemon connected, so the run halted `awaiting_daemon`\n    -> `awaiting_daemon` is NEUTRAL, so the schedule re-fired nightly, for ever, uncounted,\n       telling the owner to connect a machine that no longer existed.\n\nOn `main` that same case mapped to `failed` and auto-paused after 20 fires. The branch turned a\nBOUNDED dead end into an UNBOUNDED one - the exact pathology its own retirement fix removes.\n\nTHE FIX IS ONE DISTINCTION, APPLIED IN THREE PLACES. The `EgressCandidateResolver` seam answers\n`EgressCandidate[] | null`: `null` = NO LISTING (the UNBOUND default; ignorance, which may never\nescalate a neutral wait into a terminal halt), `[]` = THE REGISTRY SAYS THIS ORG HAS NO MACHINES.\n`machineRetired(id, null)` is false and `machineRetired(id, [])` is true. `resolveLocality` turns a\n\"start your machine\" refusal into a TERMINAL one when the listing is known-empty and no daemon is\nconnected, because there is nothing to start. A store error still THROWS rather than answering\n`null`: a throw is a terminal run failure and therefore bounded.\n\n`clearedBy` NOW NAMES THE ACT, NOT THE ACTOR: `'start-a-machine' | 'pair-a-machine'`. Its one\nconsumer has to pick a halt with it, and \"a person must do something\" was never enough to pick one.\n`pair-a-machine` becomes the ceremony halt when the step DECLARES a credential (the solo-tenant case:\npair a machine, then establish the session) and a plain non-recoverable failure when it does not - a\ncredential ask for a step that wants none is a wrong specific instruction, which is worse than an\nhonest general one.\n\nWHAT CAME OUT, all of it reachable only from a test:\n - `resolveLocality`'s copy of the retirement refusal. A preference is LEARNED only from a checkout\n   that SUCCEEDED, and a ceremony session is bound to its machine's residential line, so a successful\n   checkout proves the machine is still listed. `credentialGateRecord` is the only producer now, and\n   `SESSION_MACHINE_RETIRED_REASON` moved to `egress-policy.ts` beside the predicate that produces it.\n - `establishedByPairingId` on `EnsureSessionResult.reestablished`, read off `EnsureSessionInput.\n   vantage`, which has no production supplier and cannot get one while the typist is hosted.\n - the `schedule_blocked` notification's silence: emitted for every blocked fire, subscribed by\n   nothing in `web/`. The schedules page now listens, toasts the CAUSE's words and refetches.\n - `hostedTypist: {}` hard-coded in the Citius rail - the one consumer able to type a court password\n   into the hosted Chromium for an origin nobody classified. The permit is an INPUT now, composed at\n   `routes/sync.ts` from `classifyOrigin`, which withholds it.\n\nAND ONE TENANCY REPAIR. `resolveEgress` had degraded to narrowing its ROWS by a set of pairing IDS.\nA foreign row sharing one of our ids, listed first, became `usable[0]` and its tailnet address became\nthe run's proxy. `c.org === actorOrg` is back on the row filter: a boundary must not rest on an id\nbeing unique across tenants.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SIX: an EMPTY fleet is a fact, not ignorance - and four pieces of dead\nsurface come out).\n\nTHE REGRESSION THIS ROUND EXISTS FOR. Rounds three to five taught the run loop to tell a machine that\nis ASLEEP from one that is GONE, and made `awaiting_daemon` NEUTRAL against the 20-strike failure\nceiling on the grounds that opening a laptop clears it. Put those together for a SOLO TENANT who\npairs one laptop, holds an attended ceremony on it, then revokes the pairing:\n\n  `egressCandidatesForOrg` filters `revokedAt: null`, so the org's listing is genuinely `[]`\n    -> `[]` was read as \"this process does not know what this org has\"\n    -> `machineRetired` answered NO, so NEITHER retirement branch fired\n    -> no daemon connected, so the run halted `awaiting_daemon`\n    -> `awaiting_daemon` is NEUTRAL, so the schedule re-fired nightly, for ever, uncounted,\n       telling the owner to connect a machine that no longer existed.\n\nOn `main` that same case mapped to `failed` and auto-paused after 20 fires. The branch turned a\nBOUNDED dead end into an UNBOUNDED one - the exact pathology its own retirement fix removes.\n\nTHE FIX IS ONE DISTINCTION, APPLIED IN THREE PLACES. The `EgressCandidateResolver` seam answers\n`EgressCandidate[] | null`: `null` = NO LISTING (the UNBOUND default; ignorance, which may never\nescalate a neutral wait into a terminal halt), `[]` = THE REGISTRY SAYS THIS ORG HAS NO MACHINES.\n`machineRetired(id, null)` is false and `machineRetired(id, [])` is true. `resolveLocality` turns a\n\"start your machine\" refusal into a TERMINAL one when the listing is known-empty and no daemon is\nconnected, because there is nothing to start. A store error still THROWS rather than answering\n`null`: a throw is a terminal run failure and therefore bounded.\n\n`clearedBy` NOW NAMES THE ACT, NOT THE ACTOR: `'start-a-machine' | 'pair-a-machine'`. Its one\nconsumer has to pick a halt with it, and \"a person must do something\" was never enough to pick one.\n`pair-a-machine` becomes the ceremony halt when the step DECLARES a credential (the solo-tenant case:\npair a machine, then establish the session) and a plain non-recoverable failure when it does not - a\ncredential ask for a step that wants none is a wrong specific instruction, which is worse than an\nhonest general one.\n\nWHAT CAME OUT, all of it reachable only from a test:\n - `resolveLocality`'s copy of the retirement refusal. A preference is LEARNED only from a checkout\n   that SUCCEEDED, and a ceremony session is bound to its machine's residential line, so a successful\n   checkout proves the machine is still listed. `credentialGateRecord` is the only producer now, and\n   `SESSION_MACHINE_RETIRED_REASON` moved to `egress-policy.ts` beside the predicate that produces it.\n - `establishedByPairingId` on `EnsureSessionResult.reestablished`, read off `EnsureSessionInput.\n   vantage`, which has no production supplier and cannot get one while the typist is hosted.\n - the `schedule_blocked` notification's silence: emitted for every blocked fire, subscribed by\n   nothing in `web/`. The schedules page now listens, toasts the CAUSE's words and refetches.\n - `hostedTypist: {}` hard-coded in the Citius rail - the one consumer able to type a court password\n   into the hosted Chromium for an origin nobody classified. The permit is an INPUT now, composed at\n   `routes/sync.ts` from `classifyOrigin`, which withholds it.\n\nAND ONE TENANCY REPAIR. `resolveEgress` had degraded to narrowing its ROWS by a set of pairing IDS.\nA foreign row sharing one of our ids, listed first, became `usable[0]` and its tailnet address became\nthe run's proxy. `c.org === actorOrg` is back on the row filter: a boundary must not rest on an id\nbeing unique across tenants."
