      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SEVEN: neutrality stops being the fall-through, and every refusal\nmoves inside one module).\n\nTHE CLASS, after three sightings of it. A refusal that is NEUTRAL against the 20-strike failure\nceiling but REPEATS IDENTICALLY for ever. Neutrality is correct when the cause is ENVIRONMENTAL and an\nact available to the owner clears it - a shut laptop: start it, and the next fire succeeds. It is\nwrong when the cause is a property of the STEP LIST, because replaying the same steps reaches the same\nindex and halts identically. The schedule then re-fires nightly for ever, the ceiling counts none of\nthem, it never auto-pauses, and the remediation the halt prints cannot possibly work.\n\n  sighting 1   an org whose only machine is revoked                fixed round six\n  sighting 2   the mid-run ROUTE SWITCH refusal                    deferred round six, fixed here\n  sighting 3   the POSTURE-DRIFT refusal in resolveLocalityForStep found + fixed here\n\nSIGHTING 3, AND HOW IT HID. Automation [integration(posture permissive, baseUrl portal.example.com),\nwait, wait], no daemon. Permissive means locality answers `in-process` and the hosted Chromium opens.\nThe act in step 1 leaves the declared origin (a click, an OAuth hop, a 302), the post-action\nobservation reports bank.example.pt, and engine.ts built {kind:'blocked',\nclearedBy:'start-a-machine'} -> awaiting_daemon -> NEUTRAL. Two consecutive runAutomation calls on an\nidentical fixture both returned awaiting_daemon. It escaped the cross-product census in\ntests/automation/locality.test.ts for one reason: that census enumerates the refusals `resolveLocality`\nRETURNS, and this one was assembled next door. A census of one function is not a census of the product.\n  (Correction to the report: `clearedBy: 'start-a-machine'` is a false INSTRUCTION here, but a\n  connected daemon would in fact sidestep the check by making the verdict `bridge` before it runs.\n  That is an accident of the route, not the remediation, and it is unavailable to the owner with no\n  machine - which is every owner running only in the hosted browser.)\n\nTHE FIX IS THE SHAPE, NOT THE INSTANCE.\n  1  `clearedBy` gains `edit-the-automation`, for a cause that is a property of the step list. It maps\n     to the plain non-recoverable failure - terminal, drives the ceiling, auto-pauses loudly - and\n     NEVER to the ceremony ask: re-establishing a session does not stop an automation navigating off\n     its declared origin, and a wrong specific instruction is worse than an honest general one.\n  2  CLEARING_ACTS is Record<ClearingAct, {neutral, because}>. `refusalRecordFor` asked\n     `clearedBy === 'pair-a-machine'` and carried EVERYTHING ELSE as the neutral halt, so a new refusal\n     inherited \"retry for ever\" by saying nothing - which is how all three sightings arrived. It is now\n     a TABLE READ: a new act does not compile until its neutrality is written beside the reason it is\n     true, and the default for anything unconsidered is TERMINAL.\n  3  THE BLOCKED MEMBER IS BRANDED with a module-private unique symbol, so `locality.ts` is the only\n     module in the repository that can construct a refusal. Verified by the two external construction\n     sites (both in one test) that stopped compiling. The drift and route-switch decisions moved in\n     with it as `narrowLocalityForRun`, pure over the run's live facts: engine gathers, locality judges.\n  4  THE CENSUS DRIVES BOTH ENTRY POINTS, and asserts the terminal set is exactly the three refusals\n     whose cause is not environmental - plus that every act in CLEARING_ACTS is reachable and every\n     act reached is in CLEARING_ACTS.\n\nALSO: `awaiting_consent` joins BLOCKED_RUN_STATUSES. A schedule pointed at an INTEGRATION ACTION\nalready reported `blocked` + code awaiting_consent, while one pointed at an AUTOMATION that halted the\nsame way reported `failed` - so the badge said \"Failed\" for a run waiting on the owner's own approval,\nwith the correct copy already shipped and already reachable from the other rail. The word changes; the\nceiling does not. `awaiting_consent` stays OUT of NEUTRAL_BLOCKED_CODES, because nothing about waiting\nbrings an approval closer.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SEVEN: neutrality stops being the fall-through, and every refusal\nmoves inside one module).\n\nTHE CLASS, after three sightings of it. A refusal that is NEUTRAL against the 20-strike failure\nceiling but REPEATS IDENTICALLY for ever. Neutrality is correct when the cause is ENVIRONMENTAL and an\nact available to the owner clears it - a shut laptop: start it, and the next fire succeeds. It is\nwrong when the cause is a property of the STEP LIST, because replaying the same steps reaches the same\nindex and halts identically. The schedule then re-fires nightly for ever, the ceiling counts none of\nthem, it never auto-pauses, and the remediation the halt prints cannot possibly work.\n\n  sighting 1   an org whose only machine is revoked                fixed round six\n  sighting 2   the mid-run ROUTE SWITCH refusal                    deferred round six, fixed here\n  sighting 3   the POSTURE-DRIFT refusal in resolveLocalityForStep found + fixed here\n\nSIGHTING 3, AND HOW IT HID. Automation [integration(posture permissive, baseUrl portal.example.com),\nwait, wait], no daemon. Permissive means locality answers `in-process` and the hosted Chromium opens.\nThe act in step 1 leaves the declared origin (a click, an OAuth hop, a 302), the post-action\nobservation reports bank.example.pt, and engine.ts built {kind:'blocked',\nclearedBy:'start-a-machine'} -> awaiting_daemon -> NEUTRAL. Two consecutive runAutomation calls on an\nidentical fixture both returned awaiting_daemon. It escaped the cross-product census in\ntests/automation/locality.test.ts for one reason: that census enumerates the refusals `resolveLocality`\nRETURNS, and this one was assembled next door. A census of one function is not a census of the product.\n  (Correction to the report: `clearedBy: 'start-a-machine'` is a false INSTRUCTION here, but a\n  connected daemon would in fact sidestep the check by making the verdict `bridge` before it runs.\n  That is an accident of the route, not the remediation, and it is unavailable to the owner with no\n  machine - which is every owner running only in the hosted browser.)\n\nTHE FIX IS THE SHAPE, NOT THE INSTANCE.\n  1  `clearedBy` gains `edit-the-automation`, for a cause that is a property of the step list. It maps\n     to the plain non-recoverable failure - terminal, drives the ceiling, auto-pauses loudly - and\n     NEVER to the ceremony ask: re-establishing a session does not stop an automation navigating off\n     its declared origin, and a wrong specific instruction is worse than an honest general one.\n  2  CLEARING_ACTS is Record<ClearingAct, {neutral, because}>. `refusalRecordFor` asked\n     `clearedBy === 'pair-a-machine'` and carried EVERYTHING ELSE as the neutral halt, so a new refusal\n     inherited \"retry for ever\" by saying nothing - which is how all three sightings arrived. It is now\n     a TABLE READ: a new act does not compile until its neutrality is written beside the reason it is\n     true, and the default for anything unconsidered is TERMINAL.\n  3  THE BLOCKED MEMBER IS BRANDED with a module-private unique symbol, so `locality.ts` is the only\n     module in the repository that can construct a refusal. Verified by the two external construction\n     sites (both in one test) that stopped compiling. The drift and route-switch decisions moved in\n     with it as `narrowLocalityForRun`, pure over the run's live facts: engine gathers, locality judges.\n  4  THE CENSUS DRIVES BOTH ENTRY POINTS, and asserts the terminal set is exactly the three refusals\n     whose cause is not environmental - plus that every act in CLEARING_ACTS is reachable and every\n     act reached is in CLEARING_ACTS.\n\nALSO: `awaiting_consent` joins BLOCKED_RUN_STATUSES. A schedule pointed at an INTEGRATION ACTION\nalready reported `blocked` + code awaiting_consent, while one pointed at an AUTOMATION that halted the\nsame way reported `failed` - so the badge said \"Failed\" for a run waiting on the owner's own approval,\nwith the correct copy already shipped and already reachable from the other rail. The word changes; the\nceiling does not. `awaiting_consent` stays OUT of NEUTRAL_BLOCKED_CODES, because nothing about waiting\nbrings an approval closer.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND SEVEN: neutrality stops being the fall-through, and every refusal\nmoves inside one module).\n\nTHE CLASS, after three sightings of it. A refusal that is NEUTRAL against the 20-strike failure\nceiling but REPEATS IDENTICALLY for ever. Neutrality is correct when the cause is ENVIRONMENTAL and an\nact available to the owner clears it - a shut laptop: start it, and the next fire succeeds. It is\nwrong when the cause is a property of the STEP LIST, because replaying the same steps reaches the same\nindex and halts identically. The schedule then re-fires nightly for ever, the ceiling counts none of\nthem, it never auto-pauses, and the remediation the halt prints cannot possibly work.\n\n  sighting 1   an org whose only machine is revoked                fixed round six\n  sighting 2   the mid-run ROUTE SWITCH refusal                    deferred round six, fixed here\n  sighting 3   the POSTURE-DRIFT refusal in resolveLocalityForStep found + fixed here\n\nSIGHTING 3, AND HOW IT HID. Automation [integration(posture permissive, baseUrl portal.example.com),\nwait, wait], no daemon. Permissive means locality answers `in-process` and the hosted Chromium opens.\nThe act in step 1 leaves the declared origin (a click, an OAuth hop, a 302), the post-action\nobservation reports bank.example.pt, and engine.ts built {kind:'blocked',\nclearedBy:'start-a-machine'} -> awaiting_daemon -> NEUTRAL. Two consecutive runAutomation calls on an\nidentical fixture both returned awaiting_daemon. It escaped the cross-product census in\ntests/automation/locality.test.ts for one reason: that census enumerates the refusals `resolveLocality`\nRETURNS, and this one was assembled next door. A census of one function is not a census of the product.\n  (Correction to the report: `clearedBy: 'start-a-machine'` is a false INSTRUCTION here, but a\n  connected daemon would in fact sidestep the check by making the verdict `bridge` before it runs.\n  That is an accident of the route, not the remediation, and it is unavailable to the owner with no\n  machine - which is every owner running only in the hosted browser.)\n\nTHE FIX IS THE SHAPE, NOT THE INSTANCE.\n  1  `clearedBy` gains `edit-the-automation`, for a cause that is a property of the step list. It maps\n     to the plain non-recoverable failure - terminal, drives the ceiling, auto-pauses loudly - and\n     NEVER to the ceremony ask: re-establishing a session does not stop an automation navigating off\n     its declared origin, and a wrong specific instruction is worse than an honest general one.\n  2  CLEARING_ACTS is Record<ClearingAct, {neutral, because}>. `refusalRecordFor` asked\n     `clearedBy === 'pair-a-machine'` and carried EVERYTHING ELSE as the neutral halt, so a new refusal\n     inherited \"retry for ever\" by saying nothing - which is how all three sightings arrived. It is now\n     a TABLE READ: a new act does not compile until its neutrality is written beside the reason it is\n     true, and the default for anything unconsidered is TERMINAL.\n  3  THE BLOCKED MEMBER IS BRANDED with a module-private unique symbol, so `locality.ts` is the only\n     module in the repository that can construct a refusal. Verified by the two external construction\n     sites (both in one test) that stopped compiling. The drift and route-switch decisions moved in\n     with it as `narrowLocalityForRun`, pure over the run's live facts: engine gathers, locality judges.\n  4  THE CENSUS DRIVES BOTH ENTRY POINTS, and asserts the terminal set is exactly the three refusals\n     whose cause is not environmental - plus that every act in CLEARING_ACTS is reachable and every\n     act reached is in CLEARING_ACTS.\n\nALSO: `awaiting_consent` joins BLOCKED_RUN_STATUSES. A schedule pointed at an INTEGRATION ACTION\nalready reported `blocked` + code awaiting_consent, while one pointed at an AUTOMATION that halted the\nsame way reported `failed` - so the badge said \"Failed\" for a run waiting on the owner's own approval,\nwith the correct copy already shipped and already reachable from the other rail. The word changes; the\nceiling does not. `awaiting_consent` stays OUT of NEUTRAL_BLOCKED_CODES, because nothing about waiting\nbrings an approval closer."
