      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: neutrality brings its own bound - a cooldown, not a pause).\n\nTHE DEBT THE EXEMPTION LEFT. Round one took `awaiting_daemon` out of the failure ceiling, correctly:\ntwenty nights with the laptop shut must not auto-pause a working schedule. But the ceiling was the\nonly cap on REPEATING a block, and nothing replaced it. A per-minute schedule pointed at a\nbridge-only automation with no daemon connected fired 1440 times a day, for ever -\n  ~1440 scheduleRuns rows + ~1440 automation-run rows (neither store has retention)\n  1440 `schedule_blocked` notifications - the compensating measure, itself unbounded\nand every one of those fires produced the identical outcome. On main the ceiling stopped it after 20.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER. A neutral halt is BY DEFINITION one that waiting fixes, so the bound is\nmore waiting rather than a count:\n\n  schedules/store.ts   ScheduleDoc gains consecutiveNeutralBlocks, neutralBackoffUntil,\n                       lastNeutralNotifiedAt (all optional; additive)\n  supervisor.ts        neutralBackoffMs(n) = min(60s * 2^(n-1), 15min)\n                       recordOutcome  -> on a neutral fire: streak++, stamp the cooldown, and\n                                         return whether to notify. Any non-neutral outcome resets\n                                         the streak and clears the cooldown.\n                       claimAndFire   -> while neutralBackoffUntil is in the future, advance the\n                                         pointer past it and claim NOTHING: no run row, no\n                                         automation run, no notification, no trace at all.\n                       coolingUntilMs -> the ONE expression both readers share. A cooling\n                                         schedule is also EXEMPT from the concurrency gate:\n                                         it fires nothing, so by that gate's own rule it must\n                                         not queue behind a slow automation. Fails OPEN on an\n                                         unparseable stamp - a cooldown nobody can read must\n                                         not stop a schedule for ever.\n                       execute        -> notifies only when recordOutcome says so\n  schedules/service.ts an owner re-enabling clears the streak + cooldown, as it clears the ceiling\n\n  per-minute schedule, no daemon:  ~2880 rows + 1440 pushes/day  ->  ~96 rows + 2 pushes/day\n  resume latency after the laptop opens:                             <= 15 minutes\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT COOLED. A NON-neutral block (`needs_credentials`, `awaiting_consent`)\nfires every occurrence exactly as before. It is already capped - by the ceiling - and slowing it\nwould delay the auto-pause that is how its owner finds out anything is wrong.\n\nWHY 15 MINUTES. Below any cadence a person authors by hand, so an hourly or nightly schedule never\nobserves this mechanism at all: its own next occurrence is always further out than the cooldown. It\nbites only schedules firing faster than they could possibly be unblocked - which is the unbounded\ncase and nothing else. The schedule stays ENABLED throughout: the state being waited on is real and\nthe wait is correct; only its cost is capped.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: neutrality brings its own bound - a cooldown, not a pause).\n\nTHE DEBT THE EXEMPTION LEFT. Round one took `awaiting_daemon` out of the failure ceiling, correctly:\ntwenty nights with the laptop shut must not auto-pause a working schedule. But the ceiling was the\nonly cap on REPEATING a block, and nothing replaced it. A per-minute schedule pointed at a\nbridge-only automation with no daemon connected fired 1440 times a day, for ever -\n  ~1440 scheduleRuns rows + ~1440 automation-run rows (neither store has retention)\n  1440 `schedule_blocked` notifications - the compensating measure, itself unbounded\nand every one of those fires produced the identical outcome. On main the ceiling stopped it after 20.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER. A neutral halt is BY DEFINITION one that waiting fixes, so the bound is\nmore waiting rather than a count:\n\n  schedules/store.ts   ScheduleDoc gains consecutiveNeutralBlocks, neutralBackoffUntil,\n                       lastNeutralNotifiedAt (all optional; additive)\n  supervisor.ts        neutralBackoffMs(n) = min(60s * 2^(n-1), 15min)\n                       recordOutcome  -> on a neutral fire: streak++, stamp the cooldown, and\n                                         return whether to notify. Any non-neutral outcome resets\n                                         the streak and clears the cooldown.\n                       claimAndFire   -> while neutralBackoffUntil is in the future, advance the\n                                         pointer past it and claim NOTHING: no run row, no\n                                         automation run, no notification, no trace at all.\n                       coolingUntilMs -> the ONE expression both readers share. A cooling\n                                         schedule is also EXEMPT from the concurrency gate:\n                                         it fires nothing, so by that gate's own rule it must\n                                         not queue behind a slow automation. Fails OPEN on an\n                                         unparseable stamp - a cooldown nobody can read must\n                                         not stop a schedule for ever.\n                       execute        -> notifies only when recordOutcome says so\n  schedules/service.ts an owner re-enabling clears the streak + cooldown, as it clears the ceiling\n\n  per-minute schedule, no daemon:  ~2880 rows + 1440 pushes/day  ->  ~96 rows + 2 pushes/day\n  resume latency after the laptop opens:                             <= 15 minutes\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT COOLED. A NON-neutral block (`needs_credentials`, `awaiting_consent`)\nfires every occurrence exactly as before. It is already capped - by the ceiling - and slowing it\nwould delay the auto-pause that is how its owner finds out anything is wrong.\n\nWHY 15 MINUTES. Below any cadence a person authors by hand, so an hourly or nightly schedule never\nobserves this mechanism at all: its own next occurrence is always further out than the cooldown. It\nbites only schedules firing faster than they could possibly be unblocked - which is the unbounded\ncase and nothing else. The schedule stays ENABLED throughout: the state being waited on is real and\nthe wait is correct; only its cost is capped.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: neutrality brings its own bound - a cooldown, not a pause).\n\nTHE DEBT THE EXEMPTION LEFT. Round one took `awaiting_daemon` out of the failure ceiling, correctly:\ntwenty nights with the laptop shut must not auto-pause a working schedule. But the ceiling was the\nonly cap on REPEATING a block, and nothing replaced it. A per-minute schedule pointed at a\nbridge-only automation with no daemon connected fired 1440 times a day, for ever -\n  ~1440 scheduleRuns rows + ~1440 automation-run rows (neither store has retention)\n  1440 `schedule_blocked` notifications - the compensating measure, itself unbounded\nand every one of those fires produced the identical outcome. On main the ceiling stopped it after 20.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER. A neutral halt is BY DEFINITION one that waiting fixes, so the bound is\nmore waiting rather than a count:\n\n  schedules/store.ts   ScheduleDoc gains consecutiveNeutralBlocks, neutralBackoffUntil,\n                       lastNeutralNotifiedAt (all optional; additive)\n  supervisor.ts        neutralBackoffMs(n) = min(60s * 2^(n-1), 15min)\n                       recordOutcome  -> on a neutral fire: streak++, stamp the cooldown, and\n                                         return whether to notify. Any non-neutral outcome resets\n                                         the streak and clears the cooldown.\n                       claimAndFire   -> while neutralBackoffUntil is in the future, advance the\n                                         pointer past it and claim NOTHING: no run row, no\n                                         automation run, no notification, no trace at all.\n                       coolingUntilMs -> the ONE expression both readers share. A cooling\n                                         schedule is also EXEMPT from the concurrency gate:\n                                         it fires nothing, so by that gate's own rule it must\n                                         not queue behind a slow automation. Fails OPEN on an\n                                         unparseable stamp - a cooldown nobody can read must\n                                         not stop a schedule for ever.\n                       execute        -> notifies only when recordOutcome says so\n  schedules/service.ts an owner re-enabling clears the streak + cooldown, as it clears the ceiling\n\n  per-minute schedule, no daemon:  ~2880 rows + 1440 pushes/day  ->  ~96 rows + 2 pushes/day\n  resume latency after the laptop opens:                             <= 15 minutes\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT COOLED. A NON-neutral block (`needs_credentials`, `awaiting_consent`)\nfires every occurrence exactly as before. It is already capped - by the ceiling - and slowing it\nwould delay the auto-pause that is how its owner finds out anything is wrong.\n\nWHY 15 MINUTES. Below any cadence a person authors by hand, so an hourly or nightly schedule never\nobserves this mechanism at all: its own next occurrence is always further out than the cooldown. It\nbites only schedules firing faster than they could possibly be unblocked - which is the unbounded\ncase and nothing else. The schedule stays ENABLED throughout: the state being waited on is real and\nthe wait is correct; only its cost is capped."
