      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 - P2.2/P2.3: network capture and in-page replay, as a security surface.\n\nWHAT IS NEW ON THE WIRE (shared/src/ekoa-local.ts, additive - Rule 7):\n  captureOp: start|stop      a LIFECYCLE arm of LocalBrowserStepInput, deliberately NOT a member of LocalBrowserAction.\n                             Everything in that union is something a resolver or the vision tier can EMIT for a step, so\n                             'record every request this authenticated page makes' sitting there would be one bad model\n                             completion away from being switched on. Capture is armed by the discovery driver alone.\n  injectedCall               method + url + header NAMES. Replayed by fetch() INSIDE the authenticated page, so it\n                             inherits the origin, the cookie jar (HttpOnly + SameSite), the TLS session and the IP\n                             (trap T3). A bare Node request inherits none of those - which is why that rung is\n                             restricted to origins classified PERMISSIVE, and the default classification is closed.\n  observation.captures       drained onto the frame of the act that PROVOKED the requests, so a capture is attributable.\n  observation.injectedCall   the verdict. A non-2xx is reported, never thrown: a 404 from a replayed call RAN, and it\n                             is the drift signal self-heal is keyed on.\n\nSECRETS: TWO INDEPENDENT REDACTION LEGS, PLUS A REFUSAL (trap T8).\n  1. ON THE MACHINE. NetworkRecorder builds the frame from the header map's KEYS; the values are never constructed into\n     anything that leaves. Bodies pass the daemon's OutboundRedactor, which knows what was DELIVERED to this machine.\n  2. HOSTED. network-capture.redactCaptures applies the run's SecretRegistry (what the RUN resolved) plus the\n     name-pattern legs. Neither leg is a superset of the other, which is why both run.\n  3. THE STORES REFUSE. captured-calls-store re-serialises the whole document and fails the append if a live value\n     survived; recipe-store refuses (never redacts) a recipe carrying one. A redacted recipe is a broken recipe, and a\n     capture path still writing values is a defect that has to surface.\n\nWHERE THE VALUES DO LIVE: NetworkRecorder.live, in RAM, per LEASE, per ORIGIN. That is what lets a valueless recipe\nreconstitute a working authenticated call - Cortex asks for 'x-csrf-token' and the machine answers with whatever it\ncurrently is. captureOp:'stop' and the lease release both drop the map, so it cannot outlive the jar it was read from.\nThe browser-owned headers (cookie, host, user-agent...) are never forged from it: forging cookie would replace the live\nsession with a remembered one, defeating the inheritance the in-page rung exists for.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 - P2.2/P2.3: network capture and in-page replay, as a security surface.\n\nWHAT IS NEW ON THE WIRE (shared/src/ekoa-local.ts, additive - Rule 7):\n  captureOp: start|stop      a LIFECYCLE arm of LocalBrowserStepInput, deliberately NOT a member of LocalBrowserAction.\n                             Everything in that union is something a resolver or the vision tier can EMIT for a step, so\n                             'record every request this authenticated page makes' sitting there would be one bad model\n                             completion away from being switched on. Capture is armed by the discovery driver alone.\n  injectedCall               method + url + header NAMES. Replayed by fetch() INSIDE the authenticated page, so it\n                             inherits the origin, the cookie jar (HttpOnly + SameSite), the TLS session and the IP\n                             (trap T3). A bare Node request inherits none of those - which is why that rung is\n                             restricted to origins classified PERMISSIVE, and the default classification is closed.\n  observation.captures       drained onto the frame of the act that PROVOKED the requests, so a capture is attributable.\n  observation.injectedCall   the verdict. A non-2xx is reported, never thrown: a 404 from a replayed call RAN, and it\n                             is the drift signal self-heal is keyed on.\n\nSECRETS: TWO INDEPENDENT REDACTION LEGS, PLUS A REFUSAL (trap T8).\n  1. ON THE MACHINE. NetworkRecorder builds the frame from the header map's KEYS; the values are never constructed into\n     anything that leaves. Bodies pass the daemon's OutboundRedactor, which knows what was DELIVERED to this machine.\n  2. HOSTED. network-capture.redactCaptures applies the run's SecretRegistry (what the RUN resolved) plus the\n     name-pattern legs. Neither leg is a superset of the other, which is why both run.\n  3. THE STORES REFUSE. captured-calls-store re-serialises the whole document and fails the append if a live value\n     survived; recipe-store refuses (never redacts) a recipe carrying one. A redacted recipe is a broken recipe, and a\n     capture path still writing values is a defect that has to surface.\n\nWHERE THE VALUES DO LIVE: NetworkRecorder.live, in RAM, per LEASE, per ORIGIN. That is what lets a valueless recipe\nreconstitute a working authenticated call - Cortex asks for 'x-csrf-token' and the machine answers with whatever it\ncurrently is. captureOp:'stop' and the lease release both drop the map, so it cannot outlive the jar it was read from.\nThe browser-owned headers (cookie, host, user-agent...) are never forged from it: forging cookie would replace the live\nsession with a remembered one, defeating the inheritance the in-page rung exists for.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 - P2.2/P2.3: network capture and in-page replay, as a security surface.\n\nWHAT IS NEW ON THE WIRE (shared/src/ekoa-local.ts, additive - Rule 7):\n  captureOp: start|stop      a LIFECYCLE arm of LocalBrowserStepInput, deliberately NOT a member of LocalBrowserAction.\n                             Everything in that union is something a resolver or the vision tier can EMIT for a step, so\n                             'record every request this authenticated page makes' sitting there would be one bad model\n                             completion away from being switched on. Capture is armed by the discovery driver alone.\n  injectedCall               method + url + header NAMES. Replayed by fetch() INSIDE the authenticated page, so it\n                             inherits the origin, the cookie jar (HttpOnly + SameSite), the TLS session and the IP\n                             (trap T3). A bare Node request inherits none of those - which is why that rung is\n                             restricted to origins classified PERMISSIVE, and the default classification is closed.\n  observation.captures       drained onto the frame of the act that PROVOKED the requests, so a capture is attributable.\n  observation.injectedCall   the verdict. A non-2xx is reported, never thrown: a 404 from a replayed call RAN, and it\n                             is the drift signal self-heal is keyed on.\n\nSECRETS: TWO INDEPENDENT REDACTION LEGS, PLUS A REFUSAL (trap T8).\n  1. ON THE MACHINE. NetworkRecorder builds the frame from the header map's KEYS; the values are never constructed into\n     anything that leaves. Bodies pass the daemon's OutboundRedactor, which knows what was DELIVERED to this machine.\n  2. HOSTED. network-capture.redactCaptures applies the run's SecretRegistry (what the RUN resolved) plus the\n     name-pattern legs. Neither leg is a superset of the other, which is why both run.\n  3. THE STORES REFUSE. captured-calls-store re-serialises the whole document and fails the append if a live value\n     survived; recipe-store refuses (never redacts) a recipe carrying one. A redacted recipe is a broken recipe, and a\n     capture path still writing values is a defect that has to surface.\n\nWHERE THE VALUES DO LIVE: NetworkRecorder.live, in RAM, per LEASE, per ORIGIN. That is what lets a valueless recipe\nreconstitute a working authenticated call - Cortex asks for 'x-csrf-token' and the machine answers with whatever it\ncurrently is. captureOp:'stop' and the lease release both drop the map, so it cannot outlive the jar it was read from.\nThe browser-owned headers (cookie, host, user-agent...) are never forged from it: forging cookie would replace the live\nsession with a remembered one, defeating the inheritance the in-page rung exists for.",
