      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (S-inject: the DOWNWARD session channel - a bridge run can finally start authenticated).\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY THIS CLOSES. `session.push` carried a captured session UP from an attended ceremony and\n`acceptSessionPush` stored it as a Cofre item - and NOTHING ever carried one back DOWN. Every capture was\ntherefore write-only: the item sat in the Cofre, healthy and correct, while the next bridge run against the\nsame portal opened on the machine's own persistent profile, signed out. The hosted path had had the other\nhalf all along (`engine.ts` -> `LocalBrowserSession({ sessionState })`); the bridge branch carried an explicit\ncarve-out saying the protocol had no cookie channel. It has one now.\n\nNEW ON THE WIRE (shared/src/ekoa-local.ts, additive - Rule 7):\n  session.deliver  { runId, storageState }   hosted -> daemon. The SECOND frame in the union carrying\n                                             credential material; `secret.deliver` is the first.\n\nA SEPARATE FRAME, NOT A FIELD ON THE STEP PAYLOAD, and the reason is custody. `LocalBrowserStepInput` rides\nEVERY step of a run, so a `sessionState` on it would put credential material on tens of frames while the\ndaemon consumes it exactly once - the session is injected when the lease is TAKEN and `withRunLease`'s thunk\nis never consulted again. One frame keeps the credential-bearing members of the union countable and namable,\nwhich is the property every custody rule in outbound-redactor.ts / secret-hold.ts / session-hold.ts rests on.\n\nKEYED BY runId, not invocationId: its lifetime is the RUN's browser lease, not one step's invocation. NO\nNONCE, unlike secret.deliver - a secret delivery is single-use and REDEEMED so a replay must be refusable; a\nsession delivery is idempotent for the run it names and is authorised by the capability grant checked\nimmediately before it goes out. NO origin field - the cookies carry their own domains, and a second statement\nabout the same fact is a second thing that can disagree.\n\nTHE ORDER, on the seam (bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts): GRANT -> session.deliver -> authorise -> secret.deliver\n-> invoke. After the grant for the same reason the secret is: a storageState is credential-equivalent, so\nputting it on an unauthorised machine is DISCLOSURE and no later refusal takes it back. Before the invoke\nbecause the browser context is built WITH the cookies or not at all - there is no injecting into a jar after\nthe fact. The frames are socket-ordered, so sent-first is received-first.\n\nCUSTODY ON THE DAEMON (runtime/session-hold.ts). RAM only, never on bridge disk, never in the ledger, never in\na log - not even the FACT of a delivery, because \"a session arrived for run X\" joined against a run listing is\na statement about which portals this tenant holds sessions for. Keyed strictly by runId with NO fallback: one\ndaemon serves one owner but many runs, and a \"the only session we hold\" convenience is exactly how run B ends\nup wearing run A's cookies. Dropped at leaseOp:'release', on socket loss, and at shutdown. clearCookies at\nrelease STAYS: the durable copy lives in the Cofre and nowhere else.\n\nTWO FILTER LEGS, as the delivered-secret path has. The daemon arms its OUTBOUND redactor with the delivered\nCOOKIE values (runtime/session-hold.ts), so an echo never crosses the network; Cortex arms its INGRESS\nregistry with the same values BEFORE the frame goes out (bridge/session-delivery.ts, the H-4 ordering), so an\necho never reaches persistence, the SSE stream or the model. Neither is a superset: the outbound leg is only\nas good as the build the machine is running, and for a SESSION the ingress leg is the load-bearing half - a\nportal that reflects its own session cookie into page text produces an observation Cortex writes into the run\nrecord, durable in a way a dropped frame is not. Both legs skip localStorage on purpose: a site's scratch\nspace is full of \"dark\" and \"true\", and masking those mangles every observation the run produces rather than\nprotecting anything.\n\nTHE GENERALIZED SESSION GATE (automation/credential-gate.ts, D-ADHOC-3/4). The gate used to be driven ONLY by\na step's declared credentialRefs, so an AD-HOC run - undeclared origin, free-text goal - got nothing. It now\nfalls through to `adhocSessionReuse`, which for an ADVERSARIAL origin the run resolved for ITSELF injects an\nalready-stored session. REUSE ONLY: no credentialRef and no hosted-typist permit are passed, so ensureSession\ncannot reach the typist, and every outcome but `reused` maps to not-applicable. It therefore returns no\nhalting verdict - a run that declared no credential acquires no new way to stop. Tenancy is the run's own\nactor through the owner-scoped listing and unwrap; NO queryable origin index is added, because \"which origins\ndoes this user hold sessions for\" is a cross-user oracle.\n\nWHAT THIS SLICE IS NOT. It is the DELIVERY half only. The capture/pause half - an ad-hoc run that hits a login\nwall, halts, opens the attended ceremony and durably stores what the human establishes - is a separate slice,\nand until it lands the channel below only carries sessions some OTHER path already captured.\n\nREVIEW ROUND 2026-08-24 - two minors, both about REACH rather than custody (the review found no blockers\nand no security defects; three of its six findings were refuted).\n(1) The socket-drop custody clear was implemented but not WIRED-tested. It lived in an inline lambda inside\nserve(), so the one test named for it called runtime.clearSessions() DIRECTLY and would have stayed green\nwith the call site deleted. It is now socketStateHandler(), an exported factory serve() wires, and the test\nhands that same production callback to a REAL BridgeSocket against a real ws server and kills the server\nend. This is more than tidying: SessionHold.sweep is PASSIVE (it runs only inside deliver/get, like\nSecretHold), so an idle daemon parked in `reconnecting` never sweeps - this callback is the entire backstop\nfor a credential-equivalent value, and a reconnecting period can be long.\n(2) The ad-hoc lookup was INERT on observeNetwork (discovery) runs. armNetworkCapture runs BEFORE the\ncredential gate, and arming CREATES the browser session and TAKES the lease - so the lease-taking frame went\nout with no session and the gate then saw sessionUnresolved=false and never looked. The feature was dead on\nexactly the discovery / recipe-compile runs a free-text goal most often takes to an undeclared origin. It\nfailed SAFE (signed-out run, no leak, no cross-tenant reach) but it failed. Fixed by resolving the ad-hoc\nsession immediately BEFORE the arm, through the same adhocSessionReuse the gate uses (exported as\nresolveAdhocSession) - one implementation, two call sites. Safe that early for the reasons the function\nalready had: reuse only, no credentialRef, no hosted-typist permit, so it opens nothing, halts nothing and\nneeds no locality verdict. The DECLARED path was never affected (its sessionState comes from\ninputs.credentials before the loop) and the sessionUnresolved guard is unchanged.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (S-inject: the DOWNWARD session channel - a bridge run can finally start authenticated).\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY THIS CLOSES. `session.push` carried a captured session UP from an attended ceremony and\n`acceptSessionPush` stored it as a Cofre item - and NOTHING ever carried one back DOWN. Every capture was\ntherefore write-only: the item sat in the Cofre, healthy and correct, while the next bridge run against the\nsame portal opened on the machine's own persistent profile, signed out. The hosted path had had the other\nhalf all along (`engine.ts` -> `LocalBrowserSession({ sessionState })`); the bridge branch carried an explicit\ncarve-out saying the protocol had no cookie channel. It has one now.\n\nNEW ON THE WIRE (shared/src/ekoa-local.ts, additive - Rule 7):\n  session.deliver  { runId, storageState }   hosted -> daemon. The SECOND frame in the union carrying\n                                             credential material; `secret.deliver` is the first.\n\nA SEPARATE FRAME, NOT A FIELD ON THE STEP PAYLOAD, and the reason is custody. `LocalBrowserStepInput` rides\nEVERY step of a run, so a `sessionState` on it would put credential material on tens of frames while the\ndaemon consumes it exactly once - the session is injected when the lease is TAKEN and `withRunLease`'s thunk\nis never consulted again. One frame keeps the credential-bearing members of the union countable and namable,\nwhich is the property every custody rule in outbound-redactor.ts / secret-hold.ts / session-hold.ts rests on.\n\nKEYED BY runId, not invocationId: its lifetime is the RUN's browser lease, not one step's invocation. NO\nNONCE, unlike secret.deliver - a secret delivery is single-use and REDEEMED so a replay must be refusable; a\nsession delivery is idempotent for the run it names and is authorised by the capability grant checked\nimmediately before it goes out. NO origin field - the cookies carry their own domains, and a second statement\nabout the same fact is a second thing that can disagree.\n\nTHE ORDER, on the seam (bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts): GRANT -> session.deliver -> authorise -> secret.deliver\n-> invoke. After the grant for the same reason the secret is: a storageState is credential-equivalent, so\nputting it on an unauthorised machine is DISCLOSURE and no later refusal takes it back. Before the invoke\nbecause the browser context is built WITH the cookies or not at all - there is no injecting into a jar after\nthe fact. The frames are socket-ordered, so sent-first is received-first.\n\nCUSTODY ON THE DAEMON (runtime/session-hold.ts). RAM only, never on bridge disk, never in the ledger, never in\na log - not even the FACT of a delivery, because \"a session arrived for run X\" joined against a run listing is\na statement about which portals this tenant holds sessions for. Keyed strictly by runId with NO fallback: one\ndaemon serves one owner but many runs, and a \"the only session we hold\" convenience is exactly how run B ends\nup wearing run A's cookies. Dropped at leaseOp:'release', on socket loss, and at shutdown. clearCookies at\nrelease STAYS: the durable copy lives in the Cofre and nowhere else.\n\nTWO FILTER LEGS, as the delivered-secret path has. The daemon arms its OUTBOUND redactor with the delivered\nCOOKIE values (runtime/session-hold.ts), so an echo never crosses the network; Cortex arms its INGRESS\nregistry with the same values BEFORE the frame goes out (bridge/session-delivery.ts, the H-4 ordering), so an\necho never reaches persistence, the SSE stream or the model. Neither is a superset: the outbound leg is only\nas good as the build the machine is running, and for a SESSION the ingress leg is the load-bearing half - a\nportal that reflects its own session cookie into page text produces an observation Cortex writes into the run\nrecord, durable in a way a dropped frame is not. Both legs skip localStorage on purpose: a site's scratch\nspace is full of \"dark\" and \"true\", and masking those mangles every observation the run produces rather than\nprotecting anything.\n\nTHE GENERALIZED SESSION GATE (automation/credential-gate.ts, D-ADHOC-3/4). The gate used to be driven ONLY by\na step's declared credentialRefs, so an AD-HOC run - undeclared origin, free-text goal - got nothing. It now\nfalls through to `adhocSessionReuse`, which for an ADVERSARIAL origin the run resolved for ITSELF injects an\nalready-stored session. REUSE ONLY: no credentialRef and no hosted-typist permit are passed, so ensureSession\ncannot reach the typist, and every outcome but `reused` maps to not-applicable. It therefore returns no\nhalting verdict - a run that declared no credential acquires no new way to stop. Tenancy is the run's own\nactor through the owner-scoped listing and unwrap; NO queryable origin index is added, because \"which origins\ndoes this user hold sessions for\" is a cross-user oracle.\n\nWHAT THIS SLICE IS NOT. It is the DELIVERY half only. The capture/pause half - an ad-hoc run that hits a login\nwall, halts, opens the attended ceremony and durably stores what the human establishes - is a separate slice,\nand until it lands the channel below only carries sessions some OTHER path already captured.\n\nREVIEW ROUND 2026-08-24 - two minors, both about REACH rather than custody (the review found no blockers\nand no security defects; three of its six findings were refuted).\n(1) The socket-drop custody clear was implemented but not WIRED-tested. It lived in an inline lambda inside\nserve(), so the one test named for it called runtime.clearSessions() DIRECTLY and would have stayed green\nwith the call site deleted. It is now socketStateHandler(), an exported factory serve() wires, and the test\nhands that same production callback to a REAL BridgeSocket against a real ws server and kills the server\nend. This is more than tidying: SessionHold.sweep is PASSIVE (it runs only inside deliver/get, like\nSecretHold), so an idle daemon parked in `reconnecting` never sweeps - this callback is the entire backstop\nfor a credential-equivalent value, and a reconnecting period can be long.\n(2) The ad-hoc lookup was INERT on observeNetwork (discovery) runs. armNetworkCapture runs BEFORE the\ncredential gate, and arming CREATES the browser session and TAKES the lease - so the lease-taking frame went\nout with no session and the gate then saw sessionUnresolved=false and never looked. The feature was dead on\nexactly the discovery / recipe-compile runs a free-text goal most often takes to an undeclared origin. It\nfailed SAFE (signed-out run, no leak, no cross-tenant reach) but it failed. Fixed by resolving the ad-hoc\nsession immediately BEFORE the arm, through the same adhocSessionReuse the gate uses (exported as\nresolveAdhocSession) - one implementation, two call sites. Safe that early for the reasons the function\nalready had: reuse only, no credentialRef, no hosted-typist permit, so it opens nothing, halts nothing and\nneeds no locality verdict. The DECLARED path was never affected (its sessionState comes from\ninputs.credentials before the loop) and the sessionUnresolved guard is unchanged.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (S-inject: the DOWNWARD session channel - a bridge run can finally start authenticated).\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY THIS CLOSES. `session.push` carried a captured session UP from an attended ceremony and\n`acceptSessionPush` stored it as a Cofre item - and NOTHING ever carried one back DOWN. Every capture was\ntherefore write-only: the item sat in the Cofre, healthy and correct, while the next bridge run against the\nsame portal opened on the machine's own persistent profile, signed out. The hosted path had had the other\nhalf all along (`engine.ts` -> `LocalBrowserSession({ sessionState })`); the bridge branch carried an explicit\ncarve-out saying the protocol had no cookie channel. It has one now.\n\nNEW ON THE WIRE (shared/src/ekoa-local.ts, additive - Rule 7):\n  session.deliver  { runId, storageState }   hosted -> daemon. The SECOND frame in the union carrying\n                                             credential material; `secret.deliver` is the first.\n\nA SEPARATE FRAME, NOT A FIELD ON THE STEP PAYLOAD, and the reason is custody. `LocalBrowserStepInput` rides\nEVERY step of a run, so a `sessionState` on it would put credential material on tens of frames while the\ndaemon consumes it exactly once - the session is injected when the lease is TAKEN and `withRunLease`'s thunk\nis never consulted again. One frame keeps the credential-bearing members of the union countable and namable,\nwhich is the property every custody rule in outbound-redactor.ts / secret-hold.ts / session-hold.ts rests on.\n\nKEYED BY runId, not invocationId: its lifetime is the RUN's browser lease, not one step's invocation. NO\nNONCE, unlike secret.deliver - a secret delivery is single-use and REDEEMED so a replay must be refusable; a\nsession delivery is idempotent for the run it names and is authorised by the capability grant checked\nimmediately before it goes out. NO origin field - the cookies carry their own domains, and a second statement\nabout the same fact is a second thing that can disagree.\n\nTHE ORDER, on the seam (bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts): GRANT -> session.deliver -> authorise -> secret.deliver\n-> invoke. After the grant for the same reason the secret is: a storageState is credential-equivalent, so\nputting it on an unauthorised machine is DISCLOSURE and no later refusal takes it back. Before the invoke\nbecause the browser context is built WITH the cookies or not at all - there is no injecting into a jar after\nthe fact. The frames are socket-ordered, so sent-first is received-first.\n\nCUSTODY ON THE DAEMON (runtime/session-hold.ts). RAM only, never on bridge disk, never in the ledger, never in\na log - not even the FACT of a delivery, because \"a session arrived for run X\" joined against a run listing is\na statement about which portals this tenant holds sessions for. Keyed strictly by runId with NO fallback: one\ndaemon serves one owner but many runs, and a \"the only session we hold\" convenience is exactly how run B ends\nup wearing run A's cookies. Dropped at leaseOp:'release', on socket loss, and at shutdown. clearCookies at\nrelease STAYS: the durable copy lives in the Cofre and nowhere else.\n\nTWO FILTER LEGS, as the delivered-secret path has. The daemon arms its OUTBOUND redactor with the delivered\nCOOKIE values (runtime/session-hold.ts), so an echo never crosses the network; Cortex arms its INGRESS\nregistry with the same values BEFORE the frame goes out (bridge/session-delivery.ts, the H-4 ordering), so an\necho never reaches persistence, the SSE stream or the model. Neither is a superset: the outbound leg is only\nas good as the build the machine is running, and for a SESSION the ingress leg is the load-bearing half - a\nportal that reflects its own session cookie into page text produces an observation Cortex writes into the run\nrecord, durable in a way a dropped frame is not. Both legs skip localStorage on purpose: a site's scratch\nspace is full of \"dark\" and \"true\", and masking those mangles every observation the run produces rather than\nprotecting anything.\n\nTHE GENERALIZED SESSION GATE (automation/credential-gate.ts, D-ADHOC-3/4). The gate used to be driven ONLY by\na step's declared credentialRefs, so an AD-HOC run - undeclared origin, free-text goal - got nothing. It now\nfalls through to `adhocSessionReuse`, which for an ADVERSARIAL origin the run resolved for ITSELF injects an\nalready-stored session. REUSE ONLY: no credentialRef and no hosted-typist permit are passed, so ensureSession\ncannot reach the typist, and every outcome but `reused` maps to not-applicable. It therefore returns no\nhalting verdict - a run that declared no credential acquires no new way to stop. Tenancy is the run's own\nactor through the owner-scoped listing and unwrap; NO queryable origin index is added, because \"which origins\ndoes this user hold sessions for\" is a cross-user oracle.\n\nWHAT THIS SLICE IS NOT. It is the DELIVERY half only. The capture/pause half - an ad-hoc run that hits a login\nwall, halts, opens the attended ceremony and durably stores what the human establishes - is a separate slice,\nand until it lands the channel below only carries sessions some OTHER path already captured.\n\nREVIEW ROUND 2026-08-24 - two minors, both about REACH rather than custody (the review found no blockers\nand no security defects; three of its six findings were refuted).\n(1) The socket-drop custody clear was implemented but not WIRED-tested. It lived in an inline lambda inside\nserve(), so the one test named for it called runtime.clearSessions() DIRECTLY and would have stayed green\nwith the call site deleted. It is now socketStateHandler(), an exported factory serve() wires, and the test\nhands that same production callback to a REAL BridgeSocket against a real ws server and kills the server\nend. This is more than tidying: SessionHold.sweep is PASSIVE (it runs only inside deliver/get, like\nSecretHold), so an idle daemon parked in `reconnecting` never sweeps - this callback is the entire backstop\nfor a credential-equivalent value, and a reconnecting period can be long.\n(2) The ad-hoc lookup was INERT on observeNetwork (discovery) runs. armNetworkCapture runs BEFORE the\ncredential gate, and arming CREATES the browser session and TAKES the lease - so the lease-taking frame went\nout with no session and the gate then saw sessionUnresolved=false and never looked. The feature was dead on\nexactly the discovery / recipe-compile runs a free-text goal most often takes to an undeclared origin. It\nfailed SAFE (signed-out run, no leak, no cross-tenant reach) but it failed. Fixed by resolving the ad-hoc\nsession immediately BEFORE the arm, through the same adhocSessionReuse the gate uses (exported as\nresolveAdhocSession) - one implementation, two call sites. Safe that early for the reasons the function\nalready had: reuse only, no credentialRef, no hosted-typist permit, so it opens nothing, halts nothing and\nneeds no locality verdict. The DECLARED path was never affected (its sessionState comes from\ninputs.credentials before the loop) and the sessionUnresolved guard is unchanged."
