      "type": "text",
      "id": "adhoc-durable-note-11-20260824",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 12400.0,
      "width": 1180,
      "height": 840.0,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (the durable-halt fork and the ad-hoc capture: custody of a session nobody\ndeclared).\n\nTHE SECURITY QUESTION THIS SLICE ANSWERS. S-inject made a stored session travel DOWN to a machine.\nNothing yet put one THERE for an undeclared origin, so the channel only carried captures some other\npath had made. This closes that - and the moment a RUN can cause a credential-equivalent artefact to\nbe created, the five custody decisions (docs/decisions.md 2026-08-24, D-ADHOC-1..5) are the boundary\nrather than a design note.\n\nD-ADHOC-1 - THE CAPTURE SOURCE IS THE EXISTING RAIL, NOT THE LIVE RUN BROWSER. The halted run's\nstorageState is never read. A SEPARATE headed ceremony browser opens on the owner's machine at the\norigin CORTEX declared, and the session returns on the session.push frame that already existed,\nthrough acceptSessionPush with its three refusals intact (unknown ceremony, wrong machine, mismatched\norigin). No new secret-bearing wire surface - and the mismatched-origin refusal, the one that would\notherwise mint a valid, correctly-encrypted, now-ARMED item for the WRONG SITE, applies to the ad-hoc\ncapture exactly as to the declared one.\n\nD-ADHOC-2 - THE GRANT IS BOUNDED. until_locked is standing access and is right for a portal someone\ndeliberately connected; an origin reached once from a free-text goal gets 2_weeks and expires into a\nfresh ceremony. this_run is refused for a mechanical reason rather than a taste one: the run that\nASKS is the run that HALTED, and it returns as a NEW pass, so the grant would be spent before the\nsession was ever used. WHO DECIDES is part of the control - the REQUESTER names the grant when it\nopens the ceremony, and it is held server-side on the pending ceremony.\n\nD-ADHOC-3 / D-ADHOC-5 - TENANCY, AND WHO MAY OPEN ONE UNASKED. The capture is minted under the\nPAUSING RUN'S OWN actor and read back through the owner-scoped listVisible / unwrap the ad-hoc gate\nalready used; a run for owner A can never receive owner B's session, and the credential waiter that\nwakes the halted run matches on (org, user) so a capture wakes only its own owner's runs. A RUN may\nopen a ceremony only when three independent facts hold - an adversarial origin, a bridge-routed step,\nand a vision-detected login | captcha | mfa. The kinds NOT in that set are the control: payment and\nidentity are step-ups inside an already-authenticated flow, and signature is I8's subject, which no\nlogin ceremony may ever be offered as an answer to.\n\nD-ADHOC-4 - NO GLOBAL ORIGIN INDEX, unchanged and re-stated because this slice adds a WRITE path\nwhere only a read path existed. Nothing here can answer \"who holds a session for this host\":\nboundOrigins stay per-item, derived from the pushed jar's own cookies.\n\nTHE HUMAN'S DOOR IS NOT A POSTURE GATE, deliberately. POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/establish takes an\norigin from the caller and opens a ceremony on the caller's own machine under the caller's own actor\n- something they could do by opening a browser. A posture check there would refuse exactly the\nun-curated origin the feature exists for. The machine is resolved from the ACTOR and never from the\nbody, which is what stops one user opening a login window on another user's screen.\n\nAND THE PROFILE STOPS BEING HELD. A paused_for_user run blocked inside the loop holding the machine's\nbrowser lease and its Chromium SingletonLock, so a second run against the same origin timed out at\nthe invocation window and the paused one had to be cancelled - which discarded the very session the\nhuman had just established. The durable halt RETURNS, the outer finally releases the lease, and the\nceremony opens its own browser rather than contending for that one.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (the durable-halt fork and the ad-hoc capture: custody of a session nobody\ndeclared).\n\nTHE SECURITY QUESTION THIS SLICE ANSWERS. S-inject made a stored session travel DOWN to a machine.\nNothing yet put one THERE for an undeclared origin, so the channel only carried captures some other\npath had made. This closes that - and the moment a RUN can cause a credential-equivalent artefact to\nbe created, the five custody decisions (docs/decisions.md 2026-08-24, D-ADHOC-1..5) are the boundary\nrather than a design note.\n\nD-ADHOC-1 - THE CAPTURE SOURCE IS THE EXISTING RAIL, NOT THE LIVE RUN BROWSER. The halted run's\nstorageState is never read. A SEPARATE headed ceremony browser opens on the owner's machine at the\norigin CORTEX declared, and the session returns on the session.push frame that already existed,\nthrough acceptSessionPush with its three refusals intact (unknown ceremony, wrong machine, mismatched\norigin). No new secret-bearing wire surface - and the mismatched-origin refusal, the one that would\notherwise mint a valid, correctly-encrypted, now-ARMED item for the WRONG SITE, applies to the ad-hoc\ncapture exactly as to the declared one.\n\nD-ADHOC-2 - THE GRANT IS BOUNDED. until_locked is standing access and is right for a portal someone\ndeliberately connected; an origin reached once from a free-text goal gets 2_weeks and expires into a\nfresh ceremony. this_run is refused for a mechanical reason rather than a taste one: the run that\nASKS is the run that HALTED, and it returns as a NEW pass, so the grant would be spent before the\nsession was ever used. WHO DECIDES is part of the control - the REQUESTER names the grant when it\nopens the ceremony, and it is held server-side on the pending ceremony.\n\nD-ADHOC-3 / D-ADHOC-5 - TENANCY, AND WHO MAY OPEN ONE UNASKED. The capture is minted under the\nPAUSING RUN'S OWN actor and read back through the owner-scoped listVisible / unwrap the ad-hoc gate\nalready used; a run for owner A can never receive owner B's session, and the credential waiter that\nwakes the halted run matches on (org, user) so a capture wakes only its own owner's runs. A RUN may\nopen a ceremony only when three independent facts hold - an adversarial origin, a bridge-routed step,\nand a vision-detected login | captcha | mfa. The kinds NOT in that set are the control: payment and\nidentity are step-ups inside an already-authenticated flow, and signature is I8's subject, which no\nlogin ceremony may ever be offered as an answer to.\n\nD-ADHOC-4 - NO GLOBAL ORIGIN INDEX, unchanged and re-stated because this slice adds a WRITE path\nwhere only a read path existed. Nothing here can answer \"who holds a session for this host\":\nboundOrigins stay per-item, derived from the pushed jar's own cookies.\n\nTHE HUMAN'S DOOR IS NOT A POSTURE GATE, deliberately. POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/establish takes an\norigin from the caller and opens a ceremony on the caller's own machine under the caller's own actor\n- something they could do by opening a browser. A posture check there would refuse exactly the\nun-curated origin the feature exists for. The machine is resolved from the ACTOR and never from the\nbody, which is what stops one user opening a login window on another user's screen.\n\nAND THE PROFILE STOPS BEING HELD. A paused_for_user run blocked inside the loop holding the machine's\nbrowser lease and its Chromium SingletonLock, so a second run against the same origin timed out at\nthe invocation window and the paused one had to be cancelled - which discarded the very session the\nhuman had just established. The durable halt RETURNS, the outer finally releases the lease, and the\nceremony opens its own browser rather than contending for that one.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-24 (the durable-halt fork and the ad-hoc capture: custody of a session nobody\ndeclared).\n\nTHE SECURITY QUESTION THIS SLICE ANSWERS. S-inject made a stored session travel DOWN to a machine.\nNothing yet put one THERE for an undeclared origin, so the channel only carried captures some other\npath had made. This closes that - and the moment a RUN can cause a credential-equivalent artefact to\nbe created, the five custody decisions (docs/decisions.md 2026-08-24, D-ADHOC-1..5) are the boundary\nrather than a design note.\n\nD-ADHOC-1 - THE CAPTURE SOURCE IS THE EXISTING RAIL, NOT THE LIVE RUN BROWSER. The halted run's\nstorageState is never read. A SEPARATE headed ceremony browser opens on the owner's machine at the\norigin CORTEX declared, and the session returns on the session.push frame that already existed,\nthrough acceptSessionPush with its three refusals intact (unknown ceremony, wrong machine, mismatched\norigin). No new secret-bearing wire surface - and the mismatched-origin refusal, the one that would\notherwise mint a valid, correctly-encrypted, now-ARMED item for the WRONG SITE, applies to the ad-hoc\ncapture exactly as to the declared one.\n\nD-ADHOC-2 - THE GRANT IS BOUNDED. until_locked is standing access and is right for a portal someone\ndeliberately connected; an origin reached once from a free-text goal gets 2_weeks and expires into a\nfresh ceremony. this_run is refused for a mechanical reason rather than a taste one: the run that\nASKS is the run that HALTED, and it returns as a NEW pass, so the grant would be spent before the\nsession was ever used. WHO DECIDES is part of the control - the REQUESTER names the grant when it\nopens the ceremony, and it is held server-side on the pending ceremony.\n\nD-ADHOC-3 / D-ADHOC-5 - TENANCY, AND WHO MAY OPEN ONE UNASKED. The capture is minted under the\nPAUSING RUN'S OWN actor and read back through the owner-scoped listVisible / unwrap the ad-hoc gate\nalready used; a run for owner A can never receive owner B's session, and the credential waiter that\nwakes the halted run matches on (org, user) so a capture wakes only its own owner's runs. A RUN may\nopen a ceremony only when three independent facts hold - an adversarial origin, a bridge-routed step,\nand a vision-detected login | captcha | mfa. The kinds NOT in that set are the control: payment and\nidentity are step-ups inside an already-authenticated flow, and signature is I8's subject, which no\nlogin ceremony may ever be offered as an answer to.\n\nD-ADHOC-4 - NO GLOBAL ORIGIN INDEX, unchanged and re-stated because this slice adds a WRITE path\nwhere only a read path existed. Nothing here can answer \"who holds a session for this host\":\nboundOrigins stay per-item, derived from the pushed jar's own cookies.\n\nTHE HUMAN'S DOOR IS NOT A POSTURE GATE, deliberately. POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/establish takes an\norigin from the caller and opens a ceremony on the caller's own machine under the caller's own actor\n- something they could do by opening a browser. A posture check there would refuse exactly the\nun-curated origin the feature exists for. The machine is resolved from the ACTOR and never from the\nbody, which is what stops one user opening a login window on another user's screen.\n\nAND THE PROFILE STOPS BEING HELD. A paused_for_user run blocked inside the loop holding the machine's\nbrowser lease and its Chromium SingletonLock, so a second run against the same origin timed out at\nthe invocation window and the paused one had to be cancelled - which discarded the very session the\nhuman had just established. The durable halt RETURNS, the outer finally releases the lease, and the\nceremony opens its own browser rather than contending for that one.",
