    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "adhoc-capture-review-note-11-20260824",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 13400.0,
      "width": 1180,
      "height": 770,
      "text": "REVIEW ROUND 2026-08-24 (custody-scoped): the attended capture's BINDING now comes from the\nceremony, not from the jar.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. acceptSessionPush derived boundOrigins from originsFromStorageState - EVERY cookie\ndomain in the pushed jar - and never intersected it with the ceremony's own origin. The rail's only\norigin check compares ceremony.origin to input.origin, a field the DAEMON declares, so it constrained\nnothing about what the item was usable FOR: a confused or compromised machine could agree with itself\nabout input.origin and push a jar for somewhere else, minting a valid, encrypted item bound to the\nwrong site. An HONEST daemon produced the softer version, because a real login leaves analytics, CDN,\nSSO and parent-domain cookies beside the portal's own.\n\nWHY THIS SLICE MADE IT LIVE. It was latent while the rail minted LOCKED items. The ad-hoc capture is\nARMED with a 14-day grant, so an over-bound item became immediately unwrappable - and wakeable, since\ncredential-waiters matches on the same over-broad boundOrigins - across every domain in the jar, for\nan origin nobody curated.\n\nTHE FIX. boundOriginsForEstablishedHost(storageState, ceremony.origin): the same function and the\nsame matcher the typist's capture path already used to avoid exactly this. It binds to the login's OWN auth-domain\nfamily (REFINED, D-BIND-FAMILY 2026-08-26) and uses the jar only as EVIDENCE; no cookie covering\nthe host means [], and captureSessionToCofre refuses the push. That is the origin cross-check the field comparison only\npretended to be. It tightens card_login too, so the GRANT is now the only difference between the two\nerrands.\n\nALSO IN THIS ROUND. The S-login-step guard was RUN-scoped where the durable fork is ORIGIN-scoped, so\na run holding portal A's session could answer a sign-in wall at portal B with it; the gate's ready\nverdict now carries the origin it checked out for, and the guard requires exact host equality with\nthe step's own origin. And the vision validator was silently DROPPING kind='signature' although both\nprompts ask for it by name - so the I8 exclusion in CEREMONY_CLEARABLE_HUMAN_ACTIONS was unreachable\nand its test was guarding nothing. The validator accepts it now; the exclusion is a branch a test can\nenter, and both halves are mutation-proven.\n\nREFINED 2026-08-26 (D-BIND-FAMILY + D-CEREMONY-REALCHROME). Two changes from the live Uber Eats\nrun. (1) BINDING. 'the one host' broke multi-domain logins: Uber Eats authenticates across\nubereats.com AND uber.com/auth.uber.com, so a session bound to www.ubereats.com alone was\nun-DISCOVERABLE at auth.uber.com and the run re-halted needs_credentials. boundOriginsForEstablishedHost\nnow binds to the domains the SERVER scoped a session cookie to - every httpOnly cookie's domain plus\nthe established host - so ubereats.com AND uber.com, never the JS-set analytics domains (google-analytics.com\nstays excluded, the narrow property preserved). The whole jar was always stored+injected; boundOrigins\ngates DISCOVERY, not injection. (2) CEREMONY WINDOW. The attended ceremony now opens a NORMAL persistent\nreal-Chrome window (browser/chrome-launch.ts, channel:'chrome', dedicated per-origin profile under\n<home>/ceremony-profiles, --enable-automation infobar suppressed) navigated ONCE - not throwaway\nbundled Chromium in a fresh context - so capture and replay share one real-Chrome environment and the\nlogin persists. Chrome 136 blocks CDP on the user's DEFAULT profile (the infostealer hole), so a\ndedicated profile is the floor without an extension.",
      "originalText": "REVIEW ROUND 2026-08-24 (custody-scoped): the attended capture's BINDING now comes from the\nceremony, not from the jar.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. acceptSessionPush derived boundOrigins from originsFromStorageState - EVERY cookie\ndomain in the pushed jar - and never intersected it with the ceremony's own origin. The rail's only\norigin check compares ceremony.origin to input.origin, a field the DAEMON declares, so it constrained\nnothing about what the item was usable FOR: a confused or compromised machine could agree with itself\nabout input.origin and push a jar for somewhere else, minting a valid, encrypted item bound to the\nwrong site. An HONEST daemon produced the softer version, because a real login leaves analytics, CDN,\nSSO and parent-domain cookies beside the portal's own.\n\nWHY THIS SLICE MADE IT LIVE. It was latent while the rail minted LOCKED items. The ad-hoc capture is\nARMED with a 14-day grant, so an over-bound item became immediately unwrappable - and wakeable, since\ncredential-waiters matches on the same over-broad boundOrigins - across every domain in the jar, for\nan origin nobody curated.\n\nTHE FIX. boundOriginsForEstablishedHost(storageState, ceremony.origin): the same function and the\nsame matcher the typist's capture path already used to avoid exactly this. It narrows to the one host\nCORTEX named and uses the jar only as EVIDENCE; no cookie covering that host means [], and\ncaptureSessionToCofre refuses the push. That is the origin cross-check the field comparison only\npretended to be. It tightens card_login too, so the GRANT is now the only difference between the two\nerrands.\n\nALSO IN THIS ROUND. The S-login-step guard was RUN-scoped where the durable fork is ORIGIN-scoped, so\na run holding portal A's session could answer a sign-in wall at portal B with it; the gate's ready\nverdict now carries the origin it checked out for, and the guard requires exact host equality with\nthe step's own origin. And the vision validator was silently DROPPING kind='signature' although both\nprompts ask for it by name - so the I8 exclusion in CEREMONY_CLEARABLE_HUMAN_ACTIONS was unreachable\nand its test was guarding nothing. The validator accepts it now; the exclusion is a branch a test can\nenter, and both halves are mutation-proven.",
      "rawText": "REVIEW ROUND 2026-08-24 (custody-scoped): the attended capture's BINDING now comes from the\nceremony, not from the jar.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. acceptSessionPush derived boundOrigins from originsFromStorageState - EVERY cookie\ndomain in the pushed jar - and never intersected it with the ceremony's own origin. The rail's only\norigin check compares ceremony.origin to input.origin, a field the DAEMON declares, so it constrained\nnothing about what the item was usable FOR: a confused or compromised machine could agree with itself\nabout input.origin and push a jar for somewhere else, minting a valid, encrypted item bound to the\nwrong site. An HONEST daemon produced the softer version, because a real login leaves analytics, CDN,\nSSO and parent-domain cookies beside the portal's own.\n\nWHY THIS SLICE MADE IT LIVE. It was latent while the rail minted LOCKED items. The ad-hoc capture is\nARMED with a 14-day grant, so an over-bound item became immediately unwrappable - and wakeable, since\ncredential-waiters matches on the same over-broad boundOrigins - across every domain in the jar, for\nan origin nobody curated.\n\nTHE FIX. boundOriginsForEstablishedHost(storageState, ceremony.origin): the same function and the\nsame matcher the typist's capture path already used to avoid exactly this. It narrows to the one host\nCORTEX named and uses the jar only as EVIDENCE; no cookie covering that host means [], and\ncaptureSessionToCofre refuses the push. That is the origin cross-check the field comparison only\npretended to be. It tightens card_login too, so the GRANT is now the only difference between the two\nerrands.\n\nALSO IN THIS ROUND. The S-login-step guard was RUN-scoped where the durable fork is ORIGIN-scoped, so\na run holding portal A's session could answer a sign-in wall at portal B with it; the gate's ready\nverdict now carries the origin it checked out for, and the guard requires exact host equality with\nthe step's own origin. And the vision validator was silently DROPPING kind='signature' although both\nprompts ask for it by name - so the I8 exclusion in CEREMONY_CLEARABLE_HUMAN_ACTIONS was unreachable\nand its test was guarding nothing. The validator accepts it now; the exclusion is a branch a test can\nenter, and both halves are mutation-proven.",
