      "type": "text",
      "id": "ceremony-done-note-11-20260825",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 14005.0,
      "width": 1180,
      "height": 665.0,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-25 (D-CEREMONY-DONE): the ceremony's capture signal leaves the window.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, MEASURED LIVE. The attended ceremony captured ONLY when the human CLOSED the headed\nwindow it opened. On macOS that window is raised on every top-level navigation and a real login\nredirects repeatedly, so a person reading an OTP out of another app never keeps focus - and nothing\non screen said the close was the capture. The operator logged in, the ceremony expired on its TTL,\n0 items captured, run still needs_credentials.\n\nTHE NEW EDGE (Cortex -> daemon), additive on the wire (Rule 7):\n  ceremony.capture { requestId }   the dashboard's \"Concluir e capturar\", relayed to the machine.\n                                   No pairingId field: the socket IS the pairing, and a second\n                                   statement of it could disagree with the first. An old daemon\n                                   drops it at its zod boundary and keeps closing-to-capture.\n\nTHE SIGNAL PATH, END TO END:\n  /cofre card -> POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/capture (auth 'user', machine resolved from the ACTOR,\n  daemon must advertise attended.card_login)\n    -> requestCeremonyCapture: finds the CALLER'S OWN open ceremony (actor + pairing + origin;\n       newest wins) and sends ceremony.capture naming ITS requestId\n    -> daemon: matches the requestId against the ceremony it is holding and resolves that ceremony's\n       finishNow (a mismatch, or no ceremony at all, is a no-op SAID at the machine)\n    -> runAttendedCeremony: races {closed, DONE, tick} instead of {closed, tick}; on DONE it\n       snapshots the LIVE storageState and breaks into the SAME push the close path takes.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, AND IS THE POINT. This is a new TRIGGER for the existing capture, never a new\ncredential surface. The session travels the unchanged session.push rail; acceptSessionPush binds it\nwith boundOriginsForEstablishedHost to the CEREMONY'S origin, mints it under the CEREMONY'S actor and\narms it with the grant the REQUESTER named at open time (ADHOC_SESSION_GRANT for the ad-hoc errand).\nThe Done path is in fact fresher than the close path: the context is still alive when it fires.\n\nTENANCY. The ceremony is RESOLVED FROM THE CALLER and never named by them - there is no requestId on\nthe request. A client-supplied one would need checking against the ceremony's actor anyway, so keying\nthe lookup on the actor removes the check rather than passing it (D-ADHOC-3 unchanged).\n\nTHE CLOSE PATH STAYS as the fallback: no dashboard, no live socket, no correlation - and the recovery\nwhen Cortex's newest ceremony is not the one the daemon is holding. The FOCUS THEFT itself is\ninherent (AppKit activation; no Playwright or Chromium switch suppresses it) and is now confined to\nthe login instead of deciding whether anything is captured.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-25 (D-CEREMONY-DONE): the ceremony's capture signal leaves the window.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, MEASURED LIVE. The attended ceremony captured ONLY when the human CLOSED the headed\nwindow it opened. On macOS that window is raised on every top-level navigation and a real login\nredirects repeatedly, so a person reading an OTP out of another app never keeps focus - and nothing\non screen said the close was the capture. The operator logged in, the ceremony expired on its TTL,\n0 items captured, run still needs_credentials.\n\nTHE NEW EDGE (Cortex -> daemon), additive on the wire (Rule 7):\n  ceremony.capture { requestId }   the dashboard's \"Concluir e capturar\", relayed to the machine.\n                                   No pairingId field: the socket IS the pairing, and a second\n                                   statement of it could disagree with the first. An old daemon\n                                   drops it at its zod boundary and keeps closing-to-capture.\n\nTHE SIGNAL PATH, END TO END:\n  /cofre card -> POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/capture (auth 'user', machine resolved from the ACTOR,\n  daemon must advertise attended.card_login)\n    -> requestCeremonyCapture: finds the CALLER'S OWN open ceremony (actor + pairing + origin;\n       newest wins) and sends ceremony.capture naming ITS requestId\n    -> daemon: matches the requestId against the ceremony it is holding and resolves that ceremony's\n       finishNow (a mismatch, or no ceremony at all, is a no-op SAID at the machine)\n    -> runAttendedCeremony: races {closed, DONE, tick} instead of {closed, tick}; on DONE it\n       snapshots the LIVE storageState and breaks into the SAME push the close path takes.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, AND IS THE POINT. This is a new TRIGGER for the existing capture, never a new\ncredential surface. The session travels the unchanged session.push rail; acceptSessionPush binds it\nwith boundOriginsForEstablishedHost to the CEREMONY'S origin, mints it under the CEREMONY'S actor and\narms it with the grant the REQUESTER named at open time (ADHOC_SESSION_GRANT for the ad-hoc errand).\nThe Done path is in fact fresher than the close path: the context is still alive when it fires.\n\nTENANCY. The ceremony is RESOLVED FROM THE CALLER and never named by them - there is no requestId on\nthe request. A client-supplied one would need checking against the ceremony's actor anyway, so keying\nthe lookup on the actor removes the check rather than passing it (D-ADHOC-3 unchanged).\n\nTHE CLOSE PATH STAYS as the fallback: no dashboard, no live socket, no correlation - and the recovery\nwhen Cortex's newest ceremony is not the one the daemon is holding. The FOCUS THEFT itself is\ninherent (AppKit activation; no Playwright or Chromium switch suppresses it) and is now confined to\nthe login instead of deciding whether anything is captured.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-25 (D-CEREMONY-DONE): the ceremony's capture signal leaves the window.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, MEASURED LIVE. The attended ceremony captured ONLY when the human CLOSED the headed\nwindow it opened. On macOS that window is raised on every top-level navigation and a real login\nredirects repeatedly, so a person reading an OTP out of another app never keeps focus - and nothing\non screen said the close was the capture. The operator logged in, the ceremony expired on its TTL,\n0 items captured, run still needs_credentials.\n\nTHE NEW EDGE (Cortex -> daemon), additive on the wire (Rule 7):\n  ceremony.capture { requestId }   the dashboard's \"Concluir e capturar\", relayed to the machine.\n                                   No pairingId field: the socket IS the pairing, and a second\n                                   statement of it could disagree with the first. An old daemon\n                                   drops it at its zod boundary and keeps closing-to-capture.\n\nTHE SIGNAL PATH, END TO END:\n  /cofre card -> POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/capture (auth 'user', machine resolved from the ACTOR,\n  daemon must advertise attended.card_login)\n    -> requestCeremonyCapture: finds the CALLER'S OWN open ceremony (actor + pairing + origin;\n       newest wins) and sends ceremony.capture naming ITS requestId\n    -> daemon: matches the requestId against the ceremony it is holding and resolves that ceremony's\n       finishNow (a mismatch, or no ceremony at all, is a no-op SAID at the machine)\n    -> runAttendedCeremony: races {closed, DONE, tick} instead of {closed, tick}; on DONE it\n       snapshots the LIVE storageState and breaks into the SAME push the close path takes.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, AND IS THE POINT. This is a new TRIGGER for the existing capture, never a new\ncredential surface. The session travels the unchanged session.push rail; acceptSessionPush binds it\nwith boundOriginsForEstablishedHost to the CEREMONY'S origin, mints it under the CEREMONY'S actor and\narms it with the grant the REQUESTER named at open time (ADHOC_SESSION_GRANT for the ad-hoc errand).\nThe Done path is in fact fresher than the close path: the context is still alive when it fires.\n\nTENANCY. The ceremony is RESOLVED FROM THE CALLER and never named by them - there is no requestId on\nthe request. A client-supplied one would need checking against the ceremony's actor anyway, so keying\nthe lookup on the actor removes the check rather than passing it (D-ADHOC-3 unchanged).\n\nTHE CLOSE PATH STAYS as the fallback: no dashboard, no live socket, no correlation - and the recovery\nwhen Cortex's newest ceremony is not the one the daemon is holding. The FOCUS THEFT itself is\ninherent (AppKit activation; no Playwright or Chromium switch suppresses it) and is now confined to\nthe login instead of deciding whether anything is captured.",
