    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "ceremony-done-review-note-11-20260825",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 14750.0,
      "width": 1180,
      "height": 542.5,
      "text": "REVIEW ROUND 2026-08-25: the capture's tiebreak is gone - every candidate ceremony is signalled.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG (F3/F5). Cortex keeps one pending ceremony per REQUEST; the daemon holds one at a TIME\nand refuses every later attended.request while a window is up. requestCeremonyCapture resolved NEWEST\nwins - so in the double-open case (a person alt-tabs back from the focus-stealing window and clicks\n\"Abrir janela\" again) the daemon was still holding the FIRST and Cortex sent the SECOND: the\nrequestId could never match, Done did nothing, and the endpoint had already answered requested:true.\n\nWHY NOT SIMPLY FLIP TO OLDEST. It fails a commoner flow. A ceremony that ends WITHOUT a capture (the\nwindow closed, the login abandoned, Google refusing this browser outright) frees the daemon while\nCortex's entry lingers unswept for the rest of its 10-minute TTL; the person retries, the daemon\naccepts and holds the NEWEST, and oldest-wins then sends a dead requestId for up to ten minutes on\nthe \"that did not work, try again\" path. Every tiebreak trades one silent no-op for another.\n\nTHE EDGE AS BUILT NOW:\n  POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/capture\n    -> candidates = every OPEN ceremony with this actor + this pairing + this origin host\n    -> ceremony.capture { requestId } for EACH, oldest first (ordering is for the machine's log only)\n    -> the daemon matches on requestId: finishes the one it holds, no-ops the rest.\n\n  Not a broadcast. Every id named is the caller's own, for the origin they asked about, on the machine\n  resolved from their actor - pinned by a suite that proves the candidate list never crosses an owner\n  or an origin. It needs no prune of Cortex's map (which would delete the LIVE ceremony in exactly the\n  double-open case) and no ack frame on a rail that deliberately has no reply channel.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR WHAT THE MACHINE SAYS (F2). A requestId mismatch is now the ORDINARY case for someone\nwho opened a window twice, and a sibling frame in the same fan-out is likely finishing the real\nceremony - so the daemon's mismatch line no longer prescribes \"close the window\", which would be\nadvice against an outcome that already happened. The dashboard card, where the human is looking,\nreports what did or did not arrive, and its not-captured branch now names closing the window as the\nrecovery that always works.",
      "originalText": "REVIEW ROUND 2026-08-25: the capture's tiebreak is gone - every candidate ceremony is signalled.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG (F3/F5). Cortex keeps one pending ceremony per REQUEST; the daemon holds one at a TIME\nand refuses every later attended.request while a window is up. requestCeremonyCapture resolved NEWEST\nwins - so in the double-open case (a person alt-tabs back from the focus-stealing window and clicks\n\"Abrir janela\" again) the daemon was still holding the FIRST and Cortex sent the SECOND: the\nrequestId could never match, Done did nothing, and the endpoint had already answered requested:true.\n\nWHY NOT SIMPLY FLIP TO OLDEST. It fails a commoner flow. A ceremony that ends WITHOUT a capture (the\nwindow closed, the login abandoned, Google refusing this browser outright) frees the daemon while\nCortex's entry lingers unswept for the rest of its 10-minute TTL; the person retries, the daemon\naccepts and holds the NEWEST, and oldest-wins then sends a dead requestId for up to ten minutes on\nthe \"that did not work, try again\" path. Every tiebreak trades one silent no-op for another.\n\nTHE EDGE AS BUILT NOW:\n  POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/capture\n    -> candidates = every OPEN ceremony with this actor + this pairing + this origin host\n    -> ceremony.capture { requestId } for EACH, oldest first (ordering is for the machine's log only)\n    -> the daemon matches on requestId: finishes the one it holds, no-ops the rest.\n\n  Not a broadcast. Every id named is the caller's own, for the origin they asked about, on the machine\n  resolved from their actor - pinned by a suite that proves the candidate list never crosses an owner\n  or an origin. It needs no prune of Cortex's map (which would delete the LIVE ceremony in exactly the\n  double-open case) and no ack frame on a rail that deliberately has no reply channel.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR WHAT THE MACHINE SAYS (F2). A requestId mismatch is now the ORDINARY case for someone\nwho opened a window twice, and a sibling frame in the same fan-out is likely finishing the real\nceremony - so the daemon's mismatch line no longer prescribes \"close the window\", which would be\nadvice against an outcome that already happened. The dashboard card, where the human is looking,\nreports what did or did not arrive, and its not-captured branch now names closing the window as the\nrecovery that always works.",
      "rawText": "REVIEW ROUND 2026-08-25: the capture's tiebreak is gone - every candidate ceremony is signalled.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG (F3/F5). Cortex keeps one pending ceremony per REQUEST; the daemon holds one at a TIME\nand refuses every later attended.request while a window is up. requestCeremonyCapture resolved NEWEST\nwins - so in the double-open case (a person alt-tabs back from the focus-stealing window and clicks\n\"Abrir janela\" again) the daemon was still holding the FIRST and Cortex sent the SECOND: the\nrequestId could never match, Done did nothing, and the endpoint had already answered requested:true.\n\nWHY NOT SIMPLY FLIP TO OLDEST. It fails a commoner flow. A ceremony that ends WITHOUT a capture (the\nwindow closed, the login abandoned, Google refusing this browser outright) frees the daemon while\nCortex's entry lingers unswept for the rest of its 10-minute TTL; the person retries, the daemon\naccepts and holds the NEWEST, and oldest-wins then sends a dead requestId for up to ten minutes on\nthe \"that did not work, try again\" path. Every tiebreak trades one silent no-op for another.\n\nTHE EDGE AS BUILT NOW:\n  POST /api/v1/cofre/sessions/capture\n    -> candidates = every OPEN ceremony with this actor + this pairing + this origin host\n    -> ceremony.capture { requestId } for EACH, oldest first (ordering is for the machine's log only)\n    -> the daemon matches on requestId: finishes the one it holds, no-ops the rest.\n\n  Not a broadcast. Every id named is the caller's own, for the origin they asked about, on the machine\n  resolved from their actor - pinned by a suite that proves the candidate list never crosses an owner\n  or an origin. It needs no prune of Cortex's map (which would delete the LIVE ceremony in exactly the\n  double-open case) and no ack frame on a rail that deliberately has no reply channel.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR WHAT THE MACHINE SAYS (F2). A requestId mismatch is now the ORDINARY case for someone\nwho opened a window twice, and a sibling frame in the same fan-out is likely finishing the real\nceremony - so the daemon's mismatch line no longer prescribes \"close the window\", which would be\nadvice against an outcome that already happened. The dashboard card, where the human is looking,\nreports what did or did not arrive, and its not-captured branch now names closing the window as the\nrecovery that always works.",
